WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:02.916 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:02.916 --> 00:00:04.860 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:18.010 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.510 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So today
I'm continuing my lecture.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm starting a new
topic on online markets.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:28.870 align:middle line:84%
And in some ways, I
think in online markets,

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:36.730 align:middle line:84%
it's come to be that Google
and Facebook and Amazon

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.458 align:middle line:84%
tend to really dominate
the online space

00:00:38.458 --> 00:00:40.750 align:middle line:84%
and earn most of the profits
and most of the page views

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:41.420 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.330 align:middle line:84%
So I think most of
what I'm going to do

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.300 align:middle line:84%
is try to talk about
those leading firms.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.530 align:middle line:84%
Actually, most of today is
going to be about Google.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.197 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get a
little bit to talking

00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:53.172 align:middle line:84%
about models, how people think
about Amazon at the very end.

00:00:53.172 --> 00:00:54.880 align:middle line:84%
I actually don't know
a lot of good stuff

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.020 align:middle line:84%
about social media
other than there's

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.428 align:middle line:84%
literature about social
media addiction, which

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.220 align:middle line:84%
I think is also an
interesting topic there,

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.270 align:middle line:90%
but not much theory on it.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.830 align:middle line:90%
Oops.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.260 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, before
starting today's topic,

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do a brief
review of VCG mechanisms.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.520 align:middle line:84%
And I think Steven is
teaching [14.]124 this year.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.450 align:middle line:90%
He'll do a better job.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.930 align:middle line:84%
But I wanted to go
over this quickly

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.670 align:middle line:84%
because it helps for thinking
about allocating problems.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.710 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to find a private
value social choice problem

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.000 align:middle line:90%
to be given by four primitives.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.520 align:middle line:84%
First, you have a set of
types for each player, which

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.515 align:middle line:84%
you can think of as typically
holding their preferences.

00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:39.890 align:middle line:84%
But it could be
other information

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.200 align:middle line:90%
that the players have.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.910 align:middle line:84%
There's some
probability distribution

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.270 align:middle line:84%
p on the Cartesian
product of all the thetas,

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.690 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to think of
those p's as not necessarily

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.910 align:middle line:90%
independent across players.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.080 align:middle line:84%
There's a set A of possible
social alternatives.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.590 align:middle line:84%
In the simple auction example,
the social alternatives

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.620 align:middle line:84%
are give the good to person one,
give the good to person two,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.080 align:middle line:84%
give the good to person
three, and so on.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.720 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to talk about
online advertising examples.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.883 align:middle line:84%
The social alternatives
would be all of the ads

00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:13.550 align:middle line:84%
that are displayed
on the page and where

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.800 align:middle line:90%
they're displayed on the page.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.790 align:middle line:84%
And the complete
set of ads are all

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:18.660 align:middle line:90%
possible social alternatives.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.240 align:middle line:84%
And this model also
does other things

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.300 align:middle line:84%
like you can have
group of n roommates

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.950 align:middle line:84%
get two tickets
to a play and they

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.170 align:middle line:84%
have to pick two people
to go to the play together

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.620 align:middle line:84%
and the people could care about
who they go with and whatever.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.155 align:middle line:84%
Then the alternatives
would be all

00:02:32.155 --> 00:02:33.530 align:middle line:84%
of the sets of at
most two people

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.330 align:middle line:84%
who are going to
get the two tickets.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.310 align:middle line:84%
And then so the
utility functions

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.850 align:middle line:84%
depend on the chosen
social alternative,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:46.150 align:middle line:84%
depend on your own private
type, and then on transfers.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to make
them additively separable.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.140 align:middle line:84%
So you have this
gross utility function

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.410 align:middle line:84%
for your utility of action
a when your type is theta i,

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:55.920 align:middle line:90%
and then there's a transfer.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.380 align:middle line:84%
And so it's important that
it's a private value problem.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.470 align:middle line:84%
You know your own valuation for
all the social alternatives.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.470 align:middle line:84%
We define the utilitarian
solution to this problem

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.080 align:middle line:84%
to be the function
au mapping theta

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.230 align:middle line:84%
into a that's just
defined by au of theta

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.150 align:middle line:84%
is the action that maximizes
the sum of the utilities given

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.640 align:middle line:90%
the true types.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.540 align:middle line:84%
So again, like in a
second price auction,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.520 align:middle line:84%
the outcome that
maximizes utility is

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.860 align:middle line:84%
give the good to the person
with the highest value,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.860 align:middle line:84%
because you give it to
the highest value, that's

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.350 align:middle line:84%
the maximum of the sum of the
utilities you can achieve.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.180 align:middle line:84%
What is a mechanism
is going to be

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.450 align:middle line:84%
a way for trying to achieve
an outcome in a social choice

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:42.440 align:middle line:90%
problem.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.150 align:middle line:84%
A mechanism consists of a set
of possible strategy profiles,

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.460 align:middle line:84%
you know, one set
si for each player.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.310 align:middle line:84%
An action, so basically,
you can think of a mechanism

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.280 align:middle line:84%
as every player
chooses some player

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.190 align:middle line:90%
i chooses an si and capital SI.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.840 align:middle line:84%
And then after we observe
what actions, what

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.820 align:middle line:84%
strategies everyone
has chosen, there's

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.410 align:middle line:84%
some function for
determining what

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.870 align:middle line:84%
the action is going to be as
a function of those choices.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.300 align:middle line:84%
And then there are going
to be transfers paid.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.180 align:middle line:84%
And again, the transfer
that player i gets,

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.250 align:middle line:84%
which can be
positive or negative,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.990 align:middle line:84%
is a function of
everybody's strategy.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.740 align:middle line:84%
So if we have the social choice
problem, we have a mechanism.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:29.450 align:middle line:84%
Then we're going to
think about the game

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.750 align:middle line:84%
where players observe their
types, standard Bayesian game,

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:34.440 align:middle line:90%
players observe their types.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.860 align:middle line:84%
They then choose
strategies from the sets s

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.830 align:middle line:84%
and then the mechanism
decides which

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.820 align:middle line:84%
social allocation is chosen and
what transfers everyone gets.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.522 align:middle line:84%
And so the second
price auction is

00:04:46.522 --> 00:04:48.230 align:middle line:84%
an example of a
mechanism that implements

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.070 align:middle line:90%
the utilitarian solution.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.080 align:middle line:84%
We run the second
price auction game.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.770 align:middle line:84%
Everyone bids against each other
in the second price auction,

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:58.580 align:middle line:84%
and then in that
game, I showed it's

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.280 align:middle line:84%
a dominant strategy for everyone
to bid their true value.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.080 align:middle line:84%
And then the high
value person ends up

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.710 align:middle line:90%
getting allocated the object.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.430 align:middle line:84%
So the second price
auction is a mechanism

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:11.040 align:middle line:84%
that implements the utilitarian
solution in dominant strategies.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.820 align:middle line:84%
The first price auction
is also a mechanism

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.470 align:middle line:84%
that implements the
utilitarian solution.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.170 align:middle line:84%
There it does it just as a
Bayesian Nash equilibrium,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:22.180 align:middle line:84%
not as a dominant strategy
Bayesian Nash equilibrium.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.055 align:middle line:84%
But the first price auction also
implements the social optimum.

00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:29.120 align:middle line:90%


00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.910 align:middle line:84%
Important result due to
Vickrey-Clarke-Groves

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:36.003 align:middle line:84%
is that we can implement
utilitarian action.

00:05:36.003 --> 00:05:37.670 align:middle line:84%
The second price
auction, in some sense,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:41.060 align:middle line:84%
is just a special case of a
much more powerful mechanism.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.807 align:middle line:84%
And basically, that we can,
even in complicated problems

00:05:44.807 --> 00:05:46.640 align:middle line:84%
like the one I mentioned
with the roommates,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.790 align:middle line:84%
where everybody
cares about who--

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.650 align:middle line:84%
they may be jealous
of other people going.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.930 align:middle line:84%
They may want to go
with some people,

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:55.910 align:middle line:84%
not want to go
with other people.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.500 align:middle line:84%
Whatever preferences
you give them

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.170 align:middle line:84%
over those complicated
sets, we can still

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.410 align:middle line:84%
implement the utilitarian
solution in dominant strategies.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.250 align:middle line:90%
So how does VCG do this?

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:10.580 align:middle line:90%


00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.820 align:middle line:84%
So I had this function au, which
described what was socially

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.550 align:middle line:90%
optimal given the types theta.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:18.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to define
this second function

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.630 align:middle line:84%
a minus iu to be what's socially
optimal if we ignore player

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.050 align:middle line:90%
i's preferences.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.380 align:middle line:84%
So given a vector
of types theta,

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:32.700 align:middle line:84%
what maximizes the sum over
everybody else's utility?

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, in a
second price auction,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.900 align:middle line:84%
a minus iu is to give the
object to the person who

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.750 align:middle line:84%
has the highest value
other than player i?

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:46.380 align:middle line:84%
So this is what's the best thing
for everyone other than player

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.180 align:middle line:90%
i.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.690 align:middle line:84%
I then define the externality
that player i exerts on others

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:56.610 align:middle line:84%
to be what is everybody else's
utility when we take player

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.930 align:middle line:84%
i's utility into account and
choose the optimal action minus

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.980 align:middle line:84%
what is everybody else's utility
if we ignore player i's utility

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.470 align:middle line:90%
when we're deciding what to do?

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.900 align:middle line:84%
So generally, this is going
to be a negative number.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.402 align:middle line:84%
If we ignore player i's utility,
if we ignore player i's utility,

00:07:14.402 --> 00:07:16.110 align:middle line:84%
we're going to do
something that's better

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.150 align:middle line:90%
for all the other players.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.980 align:middle line:84%
It could be 0 whether
player i is there or not.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:22.500 align:middle line:90%
We give it to player three.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.160 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, this
is what we find.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.720 align:middle line:84%
This is the externality
that i exerts on others.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.120 align:middle line:84%
And so then definition
of the VCG mechanism.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.760 align:middle line:84%
The VCG mechanism is let
the set s be capital theta.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:41.340 align:middle line:84%
So every player is just asked
to announce their true type.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.760 align:middle line:84%
The action is the
utilitarian solution,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.540 align:middle line:84%
treating the announced
types as true.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:53.640 align:middle line:84%
And then the transfers that
every player is asked to make

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:57.990 align:middle line:84%
is the transfers that are
given to every player is

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.140 align:middle line:84%
the negative externality that
they exert on others by having

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.390 align:middle line:90%
their preferences considered.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.200 align:middle line:84%
So everyone
announces their type.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.140 align:middle line:84%
We do what's utilitarian
given the announced types.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:12.000 align:middle line:84%
And then if you're exerting a
negative externality on others

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.187 align:middle line:84%
by having your preferences
taken into account,

00:08:14.187 --> 00:08:16.020 align:middle line:84%
we make you pay for
your-- we ask you to pay

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.590 align:middle line:90%
your negative externality.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.896 align:middle line:90%


00:08:20.896 --> 00:08:24.300 align:middle line:84%
So the second price
auction is a special case.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.400 align:middle line:84%
In the second price
auction, you get the good

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.350 align:middle line:84%
and then you pay the second
highest person's value.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.240 align:middle line:84%
That's the negative
externality you exert.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.990 align:middle line:84%
Because if the winning
bidder wasn't there,

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.970 align:middle line:84%
the second high bidder
would have gotten

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.090 align:middle line:84%
the good and the second high
bidder's bid is their utility.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:43.020 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you're paying
the negative externality

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.050 align:middle line:84%
you're exerting on others,
because you're taking it away

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:47.357 align:middle line:90%
from the second high bidder.

00:08:47.357 --> 00:08:49.440 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why you pay
the second high bid, not

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.090 align:middle line:90%
your own bid.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:54.870 align:middle line:90%


00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.980 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, theorem VCG
mechanism implements

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.750 align:middle line:84%
the utilitarian
solution and is a truth

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.690 align:middle line:84%
telling dominant
strategy Bayesian Nash

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:09.450 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium in any private
value social choice problem.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.900 align:middle line:84%
So important things to
know about this, that VCG

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.150 align:middle line:90%
is just very powerful.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.480 align:middle line:84%
You can do this in models
with n goods and models

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.680 align:middle line:84%
where the social alternatives
aren't goods at all.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.100 align:middle line:84%
The social alternatives are
just things that society

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:24.330 align:middle line:90%
has to be choosing among.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:28.260 align:middle line:84%
As long as the players
know their own valuation,

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.690 align:middle line:84%
you can get players all
the players to reveal

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.760 align:middle line:84%
that information, at
least reveal it enough

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.390 align:middle line:84%
that you can choose a
socially optimal outcome.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.150 align:middle line:84%
VCG is essentially the
only way to achieve

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.720 align:middle line:84%
dominant strategy Bayesian Nash
equilibrium implementation of a.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.155 align:middle line:90%
In some ways you can just--

00:09:44.155 --> 00:09:45.780 align:middle line:84%
I haven't even shown
you the proof yet.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.700 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense, the
converse of the proof

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.910 align:middle line:84%
is if ever someone wasn't paying
the negative externality they'd

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:54.940 align:middle line:84%
exert on others, then they
could take an action that

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.150 align:middle line:84%
makes society better or worse
off by giving them slightly

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.600 align:middle line:90%
more or slightly less utility.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:06.280 align:middle line:84%
VCG mechanism is
not budget balanced.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.860 align:middle line:90%
Everyone pays this transfer t.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:11.197 align:middle line:90%
The transfers don't add up to 0.

00:10:11.197 --> 00:10:13.030 align:middle line:84%
If you think about the
second price auction,

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.830 align:middle line:84%
it's important in the
second price auction

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.260 align:middle line:84%
that the money is going
to the auctioneer,

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.267 align:middle line:90%
not to the other bidders.

00:10:19.267 --> 00:10:21.100 align:middle line:84%
If the money went to
the other bidders, then

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.017 align:middle line:84%
the second high bidder
would have an incentive

00:10:23.017 --> 00:10:25.360 align:middle line:84%
to lie and claim that the
thing was worth more to them

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.960 align:middle line:84%
than it really is so that
they got a higher transfer.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.190 align:middle line:84%
And so what happens is
the money, in some sense,

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.372 align:middle line:90%
there's a set of bidders.

00:10:34.372 --> 00:10:35.830 align:middle line:84%
The money is flowing
out of the set

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.810 align:middle line:84%
of bidders to this outside
party, which is the auctioneer.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.590 align:middle line:84%
And that's fundamental
in VCG that you

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:45.310 align:middle line:84%
can't, as I said, essentially
the only way to achieve

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.290 align:middle line:84%
dominant strategy
implementation.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.483 align:middle line:84%
And it's not budget
balanced, so you

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:52.900 align:middle line:84%
can't achieve dominant strategy
implementation in a budget

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.390 align:middle line:90%
balanced way.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:59.670 align:middle line:84%
And then in terms of is
this a practical mechanism?

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.730 align:middle line:84%
The VCG action spaces
could be very large.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:07.920 align:middle line:84%
So famous example is that
the United States, again,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.130 align:middle line:84%
in the early 2000s was
auctioning off the right

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:15.300 align:middle line:84%
to provide cellular or
telephone service in 394 cities

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.280 align:middle line:90%
simultaneously.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.490 align:middle line:84%
And Verizon was bidding
and T-Mobile was bidding

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.270 align:middle line:90%
and AT&T was bidding.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:24.913 align:middle line:84%
And they had two
licenses per city.

00:11:24.913 --> 00:11:26.580 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think
about what's the set

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.122 align:middle line:84%
of possible social
alternatives, it's

00:11:28.122 --> 00:11:32.820 align:middle line:84%
like Verizon and T-Mobile get
Boston, Verizon, and AT&T get

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:33.510 align:middle line:90%
New York.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:37.720 align:middle line:84%
Verizon and Sprint
get DC, whatever.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:42.840 align:middle line:84%
If you go with every set of
those alternatives, 20 bidders,

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.840 align:middle line:84%
so there may have been 20 choose
2 raised to the 430th power.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.000 align:middle line:84%
That's the number of
social alternatives.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.390 align:middle line:84%
VCG says you submit
your preferences

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:56.258 align:middle line:84%
over every social alternative
and then that's not feasible

00:11:56.258 --> 00:11:57.800 align:middle line:84%
and then there'll
be the second step.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.592 align:middle line:84%
You have to then figure
out, OK, if this is

00:11:59.592 --> 00:12:02.980 align:middle line:84%
everybody's preferences
over these 2 to 1,000 social

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:09.125 align:middle line:84%
alternatives, then what's
actually the best combination?

00:12:09.125 --> 00:12:10.750 align:middle line:84%
You'd have to then
search over that set

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.208 align:middle line:84%
and find what's
the best allocation

00:12:12.208 --> 00:12:13.610 align:middle line:90%
of the goods to the bidders.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.910 align:middle line:84%
It can be completely
infeasible if the number

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.495 align:middle line:84%
of social alternatives
is very large.

00:12:18.495 --> 00:12:23.390 align:middle line:90%


00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:24.560 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:28.220 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, Steven will do
VCG much better in [14.]124.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.770 align:middle line:84%
But I recognize many people
have not taken [14.]124,

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.350 align:middle line:90%
so I wanted to do it quickly.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:34.760 align:middle line:90%
Here's a proof.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.560 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you use slides
as just lecture notes.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.000 align:middle line:84%
If you want to see a proof,
there's a proof there.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.810 align:middle line:84%
So now most of what I'm going
to talk about today is Google.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:49.357 align:middle line:84%
So Google is the
dominant search engine.

00:12:49.357 --> 00:12:51.440 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, Google has
been the dominant search

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:52.290 align:middle line:90%
engine for a while.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.950 align:middle line:84%
There was a time when
many different countries

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:56.780 align:middle line:84%
had their own native
search engines

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.370 align:middle line:84%
and those native search
engines dominated Google.

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:03.960 align:middle line:84%
And so Google was, well, Google
came into the United States

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.960 align:middle line:84%
and there were four or
five other search engines

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.860 align:middle line:90%
that had 100% market share.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:11.330 align:middle line:84%
Google very quickly after its
introduction took over the US.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:13.400 align:middle line:84%
And then just one
by one by one, it's

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:15.860 align:middle line:90%
taken over all other countries.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:19.280 align:middle line:84%
Seznam in Czechia was like
one of the last holdouts,

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:22.380 align:middle line:90%
and Google passed them in 2011.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:23.160 align:middle line:90%
Yahoo!

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:27.030 align:middle line:84%
was number one in
Japan and Taiwan.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.350 align:middle line:90%
Naver was number one in Korea.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:32.130 align:middle line:84%
One by one by one,
Google picked them off.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:36.370 align:middle line:84%
Google actually passed
Yandex in Russia in 2019,

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.380 align:middle line:84%
but I guess post
war, Yandex is back

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:41.380 align:middle line:84%
to being the number one
search engine in Russia.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.910 align:middle line:84%
And then China is the one major
country where Google is not

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:49.500 align:middle line:84%
dominant, and Google just has
a very tiny share in China.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.990 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, worldwide
market share of Google

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:57.990 align:middle line:84%
was reported to be
roughly over 90%.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:00.840 align:middle line:90%
Google is incredibly profitable.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.940 align:middle line:84%
Google has $100
billion in revenues.

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:05.830 align:middle line:90%
It has very high profit margins.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.130 align:middle line:84%
It therefore makes tens of
billions of dollars a year

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.660 align:middle line:90%
in profits.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.910 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, I think Google
is even more important

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:14.220 align:middle line:84%
than just the fact that
it's dominating the search

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.500 align:middle line:84%
business is that the
fact that Google has

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.732 align:middle line:84%
this enormous revenue coming
in, the search business

00:14:18.732 --> 00:14:20.190 align:middle line:84%
lets it do many,
many other things,

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.830 align:middle line:84%
and in fact, many other
online businesses.

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:25.050 align:middle line:84%
Android operating
systems, in some sense,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.200 align:middle line:84%
Google bought
Android and puts out

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.840 align:middle line:84%
Android phones as the only
competitor to the iPhone,

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.720 align:middle line:84%
in part because it's using
the revenues that it gets

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.730 align:middle line:84%
from searches on Android
phones and the profits

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.522 align:middle line:84%
that it's getting from
its other businesses

00:14:37.522 --> 00:14:41.250 align:middle line:84%
to keep Android as a viable
competitor to iPhones.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:45.840 align:middle line:84%
Other products like Google
quickly dominated online email.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.570 align:middle line:84%
Google quickly dominated,
well, after a number of years

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.740 align:middle line:84%
came to dominate
the browser market.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.700 align:middle line:84%
Google is trying
many other markets.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:59.250 align:middle line:84%
Chrome OS and Google Docs
competing with Microsoft.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:03.330 align:middle line:84%
Google Cloud competing
with Amazon Web Services

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.620 align:middle line:90%
and with Microsoft Azure.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.900 align:middle line:90%
Google Meets.

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:09.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how well
Google Meets is competing.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.960 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, in some sense,
Google has all of this money

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.100 align:middle line:84%
and Google can plow this
money into other markets.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:16.950 align:middle line:84%
And so understanding
Google's profits

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:22.710 align:middle line:84%
are essential to understanding
what's going on there.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:27.720 align:middle line:84%
The US Department of Justice
filed suit in October 2020

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.720 align:middle line:84%
alleging that Google violated
the Sherman Act in monopolizing

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.520 align:middle line:90%
the search market.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.710 align:middle line:84%
Part of their case is that
Google makes very, very

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:36.840 align:middle line:84%
large payments to
Apple every year

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:40.980 align:middle line:84%
to be the default search
provider on iPhones.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Justice Department is alleging
that that's a monopolization,

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.080 align:middle line:84%
that Google in some
sense is actively

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.330 align:middle line:90%
acting to become a monopoly.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:50.610 align:middle line:84%
Several states have
also sued Google

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:55.080 align:middle line:84%
for monopolizing
online advertising

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:58.390 align:middle line:84%
and for their ad
sales practices.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.390 align:middle line:84%
Keep being rumors that
the Department of Justice

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.700 align:middle line:84%
is going to file
related claims also

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.595 align:middle line:84%
and hasn't happened
yet, as far as I know.

00:16:04.595 --> 00:16:07.260 align:middle line:90%


00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.630 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, some basic
facts about Google

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:14.530 align:middle line:84%
is every time you do a search on
Google, Google runs an auction.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.790 align:middle line:84%
That auction determines what the
advertisements are going to be.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:22.050 align:middle line:84%
This advertising auction
produces very stable results.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:24.930 align:middle line:84%
The advertising is
incredibly profitable.

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:28.430 align:middle line:84%
And Microsoft has been
losing billions of dollars

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.050 align:middle line:90%
a year running Bing.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:33.680 align:middle line:84%
Despite the fact that Microsoft
is channeling $1 billion

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.360 align:middle line:84%
a year or more
into Bing, Bing has

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.250 align:middle line:84%
done very little to
erode Google's profit

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:41.580 align:middle line:90%
margins or its market share.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.170 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What does
it mean for results

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:46.800 align:middle line:90%
to be stable in this case?

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.580 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Actually I guess
I'll show you what unstable is.

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.410 align:middle line:90%


00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:56.460 align:middle line:84%
So, I guess stable meaning
if you do a search on Google,

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.340 align:middle line:84%
the ads stay in the
same place and the firms

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:00.850 align:middle line:90%
don't change their bids.

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.860 align:middle line:90%
So Google runs an auction.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:06.387 align:middle line:84%
The firms seem content to
just put in their bids,

00:17:06.387 --> 00:17:07.720 align:middle line:90%
leave their bids where they are.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:10.829 align:middle line:84%
The ads stay in the same place
rather than the firms needing

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:13.560 align:middle line:90%
to cycle through the bidding.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.119 align:middle line:84%
I'll give an example of
unstable in a minute.

00:17:16.119 --> 00:17:21.780 align:middle line:84%
So I assume most people
have used Google.

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:25.018 align:middle line:84%
You're looking for
something, a turkey platter.

00:17:25.018 --> 00:17:26.560 align:middle line:84%
You do a search for
"turkey platter."

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:30.480 align:middle line:84%
And what you can get is
several kinds of sponsored ads.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.670 align:middle line:84%
You get an ad up
here at the top.

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:34.080 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you see
three of those.

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:35.538 align:middle line:84%
On shopping links,
sometimes you'll

00:17:35.538 --> 00:17:37.360 align:middle line:84%
get a box like
this with pictures.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.180 align:middle line:84%
Then you get a bunch
of ads over here

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.138 align:middle line:84%
on the right side
for turkey platters.

00:17:41.138 --> 00:17:43.180 align:middle line:84%
And then down here, you
get what people sometimes

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:46.160 align:middle line:84%
call the organic or
algorithmic search results.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.980 align:middle line:84%
These are the ones that
advertisers didn't pay for.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:52.030 align:middle line:84%
And this is just Google picking
out what's a good turkey platter

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.450 align:middle line:90%
site to look at.

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:57.820 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you haven't done Bing.

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:59.500 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, if you
do a search on Bing,

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.600 align:middle line:90%
Bing is very much like Google.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.040 align:middle line:84%
Again, I do a search
for "turkey platter."

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.200 align:middle line:84%
I get some set of
ads at the top.

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:08.530 align:middle line:90%
I get image ads here.

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:11.300 align:middle line:84%
Actually, those
are not image ads.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:14.500 align:middle line:84%
So I guess I get
ads here, images

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.380 align:middle line:84%
that are not advertisements,
ads here on the right,

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:21.130 align:middle line:84%
and then the organic
results for telling you you

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:22.550 align:middle line:84%
can find a turkey
platter on eBay.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:23.085 align:middle line:90%
Start here.

00:18:23.085 --> 00:18:27.340 align:middle line:90%


00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:31.420 align:middle line:84%
One thing you notice very
quickly if you play with Google

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:34.840 align:middle line:84%
is that what Google
is doing with the ads

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:37.940 align:middle line:84%
depends very much on the type
of query that you type in.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:41.930 align:middle line:84%
And the less commercial is your
query, the fewer ads there are.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.640 align:middle line:84%
So for instance,
I type in search

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:48.370 align:middle line:84%
for a fairly non-commercial
term, Math Counts,

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.260 align:middle line:84%
which is a middle school
American math competition.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:55.360 align:middle line:84%
And what you see is just
the mathcounts.org site

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:58.900 align:middle line:84%
with several sublinks and
then Math Counts practice,

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:01.960 align:middle line:84%
a Wikipedia article, various
other things about Math Counts,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:06.040 align:middle line:84%
but just no ads
whatsoever on that page.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And then if I make my search
slightly more commercial

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:14.200 align:middle line:84%
by putting in Math
Counts preparation,

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:17.020 align:middle line:84%
then Google decides it's
a little more commercial

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.480 align:middle line:84%
and they put two ads on the
screen up here at the top.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:22.630 align:middle line:84%
No ads on the right, but they
do have Math Counts preparation

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.370 align:middle line:84%
at artofproblemsolving.com
and amazon.com

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.780 align:middle line:84%
to get books to prepare
for Math Counts.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.320 align:middle line:84%
But then otherwise it's
still mostly online search,

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:32.090 align:middle line:90%
mostly organic.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:37.970 align:middle line:84%
And then if I picked "shorts" as
a word that's more commercial.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:41.120 align:middle line:84%
People type in the word
shorts, typically they're

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.370 align:middle line:90%
trying to buy some shorts.

00:19:43.370 --> 00:19:45.270 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
very commercial word.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:48.200 align:middle line:84%
So on shorts, you see you get
the full treatment of the 11

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:51.410 align:middle line:84%
ads, which is maximum Google was
doing at the time I took these.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:52.710 align:middle line:90%
I think may still be true.

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.420 align:middle line:90%
So I searched for "shorts."

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.830 align:middle line:84%
I get three ads at the top,
Macy's, Victoria's Secret,

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:58.880 align:middle line:90%
Zappos.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:02.070 align:middle line:84%
And then over on the right, I
get one, two, three, four, five,

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:03.570 align:middle line:90%
I get eight ads over here.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:05.840 align:middle line:84%
Other places you
could buy shorts.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:08.540 align:middle line:84%
And you'll notice that Google
has an awful lot of trouble

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.540 align:middle line:84%
even thinking of what
are the organic search

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:13.020 align:middle line:90%
results to display.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So they do put in there
was a movie named Shorts.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.090 align:middle line:84%
And so they put in a
link to that movie.

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:22.580 align:middle line:84%
They do decide that
maybe Zappos is a place

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:25.250 align:middle line:84%
to buy shorts or
Zappos, Forever, 21,

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:28.130 align:middle line:84%
and Kohl's are the places
they think of to buy shorts.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:29.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm doubting those
are the three most

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:31.200 align:middle line:90%
common places to buy shorts.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:32.340 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, that's
what Google picked out.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:35.090 align:middle line:84%
And then they also have the-- my
favorite was the Wikipedia entry

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:38.540 align:middle line:84%
in number five, which tells you
shorts are "a bifurcated garment

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.520 align:middle line:84%
worn by men and women
over the pelvic area,

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:43.737 align:middle line:84%
circling the waist and covering
the upper parts of the legs."

00:20:43.737 --> 00:20:45.320 align:middle line:84%
I find it hard to
believe that there's

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:48.260 align:middle line:84%
anybody who did the search
for shorts because they

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:49.410 align:middle line:90%
didn't know what they were.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:51.920 align:middle line:90%
And then that description.

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:53.540 align:middle line:84%
I didn't know what
a pair of shorts

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:55.880 align:middle line:84%
were, but now that I read
that, my English is such

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:59.100 align:middle line:90%
that that's what they are.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:01.310 align:middle line:84%
But it's commercial, so
that what you can see

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:04.250 align:middle line:84%
is they've largely turned the
page over to the ads rather

00:21:04.250 --> 00:21:07.460 align:middle line:90%
than the organic results.

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:09.740 align:middle line:84%
And I think what
I and others would

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:16.160 align:middle line:84%
argue is that the ads have
done a better job than Google

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:19.550 align:middle line:84%
could do, than the Google's
organic search results here.

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:21.110 align:middle line:84%
That somehow the
ads have figured out

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:23.030 align:middle line:84%
that Macy's would
be a good place

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:28.142 align:middle line:84%
to buy shorts, the Dockers,
the Athleta, AE, Loft.

00:21:28.142 --> 00:21:30.350 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, they've picked
out a variety of places

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:34.400 align:middle line:84%
where people might actually
buy shorts that, in my guess,

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.160 align:middle line:84%
is better than Kohl's,
but I don't know.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:42.460 align:middle line:90%


00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:44.990 align:middle line:90%
Just one more example.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:47.630 align:middle line:90%
This is when things didn't work.

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:51.890 align:middle line:84%
So this is a Yahoo!
search example from Yahoo!

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:56.410 align:middle line:84%
Search from before when Yahoo!
was still competing with Google.

00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:59.260 align:middle line:84%
I did a search for
"Paris Hilton."

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:04.720 align:middle line:84%
And you look at what is it
that when you search for "Paris

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:06.380 align:middle line:90%
Hilton" can be.

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:09.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, certainly you
look at what Yahoo! is

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.920 align:middle line:90%
able to come up here for ads.

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.360 align:middle line:90%
"Paris Hilton tones.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.570 align:middle line:84%
Get complimentary Paris
Hilton ringtones."

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.220 align:middle line:90%
Paris Hilton is not a singer.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.090 align:middle line:90%
She's not a music--

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:20.810 align:middle line:90%
what is a Paris Hilton ringtone?

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:24.332 align:middle line:84%
What makes-- but clearly, they
just had an ad for ringtones

00:22:24.332 --> 00:22:26.290 align:middle line:84%
that they would throw up
there almost no matter

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:27.770 align:middle line:90%
what you searched for.

00:22:27.770 --> 00:22:30.790 align:middle line:84%
And they would put Paris
Hilton in the name.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:35.980 align:middle line:84%
And then they do have the "Paris
Hilton cheap prices and huge

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:36.670 align:middle line:90%
selection."

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:38.120 align:middle line:90%
"Paris Hilton on sale."

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.970 align:middle line:84%
Again, this was an ad that
would pop up even if I typed

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:41.510 align:middle line:90%
in "shorts."

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.910 align:middle line:84%
It would say "shorts, cheap
price and huge selection."

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:45.090 align:middle line:90%
"Shorts on sale."

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:48.200 align:middle line:84%
If I typed in "pencils," it
would say "pencils on sale."

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:51.210 align:middle line:90%
Clearly Yahoo!

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:52.182 align:middle line:90%
had not figured it out.

00:22:52.182 --> 00:22:54.140 align:middle line:84%
One of the other interesting
things about this,

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.350 align:middle line:84%
though, is that you notice
online machine learning always

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:00.060 align:middle line:84%
does things that you don't
necessarily intend it to do.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:04.850 align:middle line:84%
So what is Yahoo's machine
learning thing doing here?

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.490 align:middle line:84%
It's trying to teach middle
school boys to overcome

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:11.820 align:middle line:90%
their school's content filters.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So clearly it must be that some
amount of the searches for Paris

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.620 align:middle line:84%
Hilton photos are
teenage boys trying

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:21.330 align:middle line:84%
to find naked pictures of
Paris Hilton or whatever.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:25.220 align:middle line:84%
And what it suggests is that
also try "Paris Hilton private

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.410 align:middle line:84%
parts photos," "hot
Paris Hilton photos,"

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:30.800 align:middle line:84%
because if you type in
"Paris Hilton naked photos,"

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.000 align:middle line:84%
your middle school
computer blocks that.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.930 align:middle line:84%
And so in some sense,
Yahoo! is teaching students

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:38.690 align:middle line:84%
how to avoid what your middle
school computer blocks.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.660 align:middle line:84%
Obviously it's doing it because
it notes that people type

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:44.150 align:middle line:84%
something in, it gets blocked,
and they type that in,

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:46.440 align:middle line:84%
and then they look at
something for a long time.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:51.290 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, aha, that's what I
should suggest they search for.

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:56.030 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, Google
runs an auction

00:23:56.030 --> 00:23:57.590 align:middle line:90%
every single search you do.

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.900 align:middle line:84%
You type in a search,
there's an auction occurs.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:02.150 align:middle line:84%
And the auction
occurs to determine

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:04.340 align:middle line:84%
which ads to show you in
between the time you hit

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:07.430 align:middle line:90%
your query and the page loads.

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:11.690 align:middle line:84%
So that millisecond or whatever
it is, an auction has been run.

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:17.150 align:middle line:84%
Here's an example of when Yahoo!
was running that crappy engine

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:18.360 align:middle line:90%
that I was showing you.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:23.180 align:middle line:90%
This is what bids on Yahoo!

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:26.150 align:middle line:84%
for some keyword for
click through rates

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:28.520 align:middle line:84%
looked like over
a one week period.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:32.233 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the bids
were doing like this.

00:24:32.233 --> 00:24:33.650 align:middle line:84%
What was happening
was at the time

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:38.010 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo! was running a first
price auction for ad slots.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.280 align:middle line:84%
They were running a first
price auction for ad slots.

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:42.613 align:middle line:84%
So on the word "auto insurance,"
I want to be number one

00:24:42.613 --> 00:24:43.530 align:middle line:90%
on insurance page.

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So what happens, Geico puts in
a bid and gets to be number one,

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:47.955 align:middle line:84%
and then Progressive
is like, oh,

00:24:47.955 --> 00:24:50.538 align:middle line:84%
I want to bid into penny more
than that and I'll put in my bid

00:24:50.538 --> 00:24:51.690 align:middle line:90%
and I'll get to be first.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.140 align:middle line:84%
And then Geico bids again,
puts a penny above that.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.710 align:middle line:84%
And the firms would just
play these cycling games

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.620 align:middle line:84%
where they would just overbid
each other over and over

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:03.930 align:middle line:84%
and over again, creating these
Edgeworth cycles on steroids.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:07.730 align:middle line:84%
And obviously everybody
has to write a bot

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:10.880 align:middle line:84%
to update their prices
every millisecond

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:13.110 align:middle line:84%
or whatever to keep
up with each other.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.220 align:middle line:84%
And we had this chaotic thing
where the firms were spending

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:19.070 align:middle line:84%
a lot of time
writing pricing bots

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.290 align:middle line:84%
rather than just
figuring out what

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.220 align:middle line:84%
the slots were worth to them
and putting their ads up there.

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:26.990 align:middle line:90%


00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:28.720 align:middle line:84%
So I said those
other things already.

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:35.180 align:middle line:90%


00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:41.270 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how the system works
is there can be up to 11 ads.

00:25:41.270 --> 00:25:45.470 align:middle line:84%
The way I'm going to think about
this is it's hard for Google

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:46.978 align:middle line:90%
to figure out what's--

00:25:46.978 --> 00:25:49.520 align:middle line:84%
it's hard for machine learning
to figure out which sites have

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:52.610 align:middle line:90%
good shorts at good prices.

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:55.263 align:middle line:84%
But the advertisers are going
to have information about that.

00:25:55.263 --> 00:25:57.680 align:middle line:84%
The advertisers know whether
people who come to their site

00:25:57.680 --> 00:25:59.780 align:middle line:90%
buy shorts or not.

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:02.810 align:middle line:84%
And so the idea is
going to be that this

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:05.510 align:middle line:84%
is readily available
information for Google

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:08.820 align:middle line:84%
that Google can use to figure
out which ads to display.

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:10.850 align:middle line:84%
Just ask the sellers
how much they're

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:13.050 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay to have
their shorts displayed.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.290 align:middle line:84%
That's going to tell
you the people who

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:18.470 align:middle line:84%
are going to pay more to be
displayed are the ones that

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:20.540 align:middle line:84%
are actually more likely
to sell things to people

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:21.890 align:middle line:90%
when they click on the ad.

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:23.445 align:middle line:90%
And that's valuable information.

00:26:23.445 --> 00:26:25.820 align:middle line:84%
That's information that's
easier to get out of those bids

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:29.780 align:middle line:84%
than it is out of the whole
machine learning stuff.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:34.320 align:middle line:84%
Now, in theory, what we could
do is actually figure out

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.820 align:middle line:84%
the best value page by just
having all the bidders say,

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.460 align:middle line:84%
there are 50 firms
out there that

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.940 align:middle line:84%
would like to display ads
on the keyword shorts.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:46.958 align:middle line:84%
You could just ask
all 50 of them, OK,

00:26:46.958 --> 00:26:48.750 align:middle line:84%
think about every way
that we could make up

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:53.040 align:middle line:84%
this page, which is 50
firms we could put here,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:56.620 align:middle line:90%
49 here, 48 here, 47, 46, 45.

00:26:56.620 --> 00:27:00.060 align:middle line:84%
So we just have 50
factorial over 39 factorial

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:03.870 align:middle line:84%
and then say, tell me your
bid for that composition

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:07.710 align:middle line:84%
of the page with you first and
Athleta second and Macy's third

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:08.680 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:10.840 align:middle line:84%
And then we would run
the VCG mechanism,

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:12.900 align:middle line:84%
we determine who's
best, and that

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:16.350 align:middle line:84%
would be a way to allocate
the ads efficiently.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:20.190 align:middle line:84%
Clearly that doesn't work,
because just the bidding

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:23.190 align:middle line:84%
space for the firms would
be absolutely enormous.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.970 align:middle line:84%
How does American Eagle
know what their value

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:28.450 align:middle line:90%
is for all of these pages?

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:31.260 align:middle line:84%
And then how do they
send you that message

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:33.030 align:middle line:90%
with a trillion different bids?

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:35.110 align:middle line:84%
And it's just an
infeasible system.

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:38.310 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense, theory would
say that's what you want to do

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:41.520 align:middle line:84%
is just run the VCG mechanism
to have all the advertisers tell

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:44.800 align:middle line:84%
you what's the best value
of every page to them

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.895 align:middle line:84%
and then you just pick
the value maximizing page.

00:27:46.895 --> 00:27:50.510 align:middle line:90%


00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:52.710 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what has
Google done instead?

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:57.230 align:middle line:84%
Google, again, early 2000s
invented the following.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:28:00.860 align:middle line:84%
Advertisers submit
standing per click bids.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:03.420 align:middle line:84%
So I can enter any
search query I want.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:05.480 align:middle line:90%
I could enter "Paris."

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:06.805 align:middle line:90%
I could enter "Paris hotels."

00:28:06.805 --> 00:28:08.180 align:middle line:84%
I could do "Paris"
minus "Hilton"

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:11.420 align:middle line:84%
to say if it's got Paris Hilton
in it, if it's got both words,

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:13.370 align:middle line:90%
I don't want it.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:17.300 align:middle line:84%
I can do these bids times
persons in the United States.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:21.510 align:middle line:84%
Person is 18 to 39 years
old, person is whatever.

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:23.750 align:middle line:84%
But I just have a fairly
expressive bidding language

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:26.630 align:middle line:84%
where I can say for
a person of this type

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:29.150 align:middle line:84%
having done this
search, this is my value

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:31.765 align:middle line:84%
that I'm willing to pay per
click to have my ad clicked on.

00:28:31.765 --> 00:28:35.450 align:middle line:90%


00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:39.140 align:middle line:84%
And people just can submit
these asynchronously, just

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:40.910 align:middle line:90%
any time you just have a list.

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:44.060 align:middle line:84%
Large advertisers will have a
million standing bids on Google.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:46.350 align:middle line:84%
When someone types
in a search query,

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:48.260 align:middle line:84%
Google has this
computer science problem

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:51.910 align:middle line:84%
of identifying all of the bids
that apply to that search query.

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:53.910 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, as
quickly as they can,

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:56.285 align:middle line:84%
they find all the bids they
think apply to that query.

00:28:56.285 --> 00:28:58.410 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to say,
suppose there are n bids

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:02.250 align:middle line:84%
b1 through bn that
apply to that query.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.410 align:middle line:84%
Then what Google
does is it multiplies

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:10.560 align:middle line:84%
every bid by a quality
score w1, w2 up to wn.

00:29:10.560 --> 00:29:14.490 align:middle line:84%
And then it ranks
the bids from highest

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:19.770 align:middle line:84%
to lowest on the product of
the quality score and the bid.

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:23.310 align:middle line:84%
And then chooses as
winners the people

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:25.590 align:middle line:84%
whose products
are highest and it

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:29.010 align:middle line:90%
picks some m between 0 and 11.

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:32.280 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it displays 0
ad, sometimes 1, up to 11.

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:38.880 align:middle line:84%
And then advertisers only
pay if you click on their ad.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:42.250 align:middle line:84%
So advertisers only pay
if you click on their ad.

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:45.540 align:middle line:84%
If ad k is clicked, then
what advertiser k pays

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:49.980 align:middle line:84%
is wk plus 1, bk
plus 1 over wk, which

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:52.740 align:middle line:84%
you can think of as a
generalization of the k

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:54.550 align:middle line:90%
plus first price auction.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:58.380 align:middle line:84%
So this is the lowest that
I could have bid and still

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.300 align:middle line:90%
beaten bidder k plus 1.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:03.570 align:middle line:84%
So I bid something that
beats bidder k plus 1,

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:06.120 align:middle line:84%
and they're just saying
think of the lowest

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.770 align:middle line:84%
number you could have bid and
still bid in the same position.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:11.375 align:middle line:84%
That's what we're going to
charge you on a per click basis.

00:30:11.375 --> 00:30:16.330 align:middle line:90%


00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:18.110 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to mostly
ignore them today.

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:22.210 align:middle line:84%
But the weights are
incredibly important.

00:30:22.210 --> 00:30:24.790 align:middle line:84%
I would guess Google may
spend $1 billion a year

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:26.800 align:middle line:90%
on their weighting algorithms.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:30.580 align:middle line:84%
Because the whole point is I
didn't have the other Yahoo!

00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:31.790 align:middle line:90%
examples illustrating this.

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:35.140 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo! had an unweighted
auction, unweighted first price

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:37.060 align:middle line:90%
auction in the old days.

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:42.550 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose you do a search
for the word "tennis racquet"

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:45.190 align:middle line:84%
on Yahoo! in the
unweighted auction days.

00:30:45.190 --> 00:30:48.190 align:middle line:84%
What's going to come up
first on the search screen?

00:30:48.190 --> 00:30:51.220 align:middle line:84%
You might think, OK,
it should be stores

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:52.390 align:middle line:90%
who sell tennis racquets.

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:55.180 align:middle line:84%
Like maybe Dick's Sporting
Goods is number one or somebody

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:57.070 align:middle line:84%
who is going to sell
tennis racquets.

00:30:57.070 --> 00:30:59.270 align:middle line:84%
No, that wasn't what
it was under Yahoo!

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:03.580 align:middle line:84%
It was people who sold ringtones
and people who sold pornography.

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:05.920 align:middle line:84%
And you might think, well,
why would pornography show up

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:08.295 align:middle line:84%
as number one when someone
searched for "tennis racquet"?

00:31:08.295 --> 00:31:10.820 align:middle line:84%
Well, the whole point is
it's a pay per click auction.

00:31:10.820 --> 00:31:12.890 align:middle line:84%
It's not a pay per
display auction.

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:16.420 align:middle line:84%
And so if someone types
in "tennis racquet"

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:21.910 align:middle line:84%
and then you put an ad
there that says "XXX videos,

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:26.350 align:middle line:84%
promotion $0.99 a month for
the first six months free"

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:33.320 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, 99.999% of the
people who click on that ad are

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:35.580 align:middle line:84%
not going to buy
the pornography.

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:39.362 align:middle line:84%
But conditional on seeing
the ad for pornography

00:31:39.362 --> 00:31:40.820 align:middle line:84%
and have it be very
clear that it's

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:43.880 align:middle line:84%
buying pornography and entering
your credit card number,

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:46.920 align:middle line:84%
if you click on it, then they're
going to make a lot of money

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:47.420 align:middle line:90%
off you.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:48.650 align:middle line:84%
And they're going to
make more money off you

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:50.525 align:middle line:84%
than they would make
selling tennis racquets,

00:31:50.525 --> 00:31:53.230 align:middle line:84%
because tennis racquets
have a $20 profit margin.

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.950 align:middle line:90%
Pornography is 0 marginal cost.

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:59.450 align:middle line:84%
And if you get people to sign
up for a $9.95 a month plan,

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:01.920 align:middle line:84%
you make $120 a year
off each customer.

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:04.220 align:middle line:84%
And so if you don't
have the weights there

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:07.370 align:middle line:84%
and you're only paying
when you're clicked,

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:09.080 align:middle line:84%
the screen fills
up with things that

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:12.050 align:middle line:84%
have high profit if clicked
on and that usually won't

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:15.200 align:middle line:90%
be clicked on by accident.

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:17.660 align:middle line:84%
And so anyway, the weights
there are very, very important.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:19.430 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
keyword "shorts,"

00:32:19.430 --> 00:32:22.640 align:middle line:84%
I cannot get on the Google
screen for the keyword "shorts."

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:24.980 align:middle line:84%
Because if I try to
bid for "shorts,"

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:27.300 align:middle line:84%
I can say I'm willing
to pay $10 a click.

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.710 align:middle line:84%
But they're going to
look at my landing page

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:33.420 align:middle line:84%
and my landing page doesn't look
like a store that sells shorts.

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:36.010 align:middle line:84%
They're going to be like, no
one is interested in that.

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:38.550 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to sell
the keyword "shorts"

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:41.240 align:middle line:84%
to him unless he has
a high quality score.

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:45.230 align:middle line:90%


00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:47.825 align:middle line:84%
But from now on, let me
just say from here now,

00:32:47.825 --> 00:32:50.450 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to ignore the quality
score for much of this lecture.

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:52.825 align:middle line:84%
Because you can ignore the
quality score if you're-- once

00:32:52.825 --> 00:32:56.630 align:middle line:84%
you restrict to Macy's and
Athleta and Loft and whomever

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:00.537 align:middle line:84%
who are all selling shorts, then
they may all have approximately

00:33:00.537 --> 00:33:02.870 align:middle line:84%
equal quality scores and the
quality scores don't matter

00:33:02.870 --> 00:33:03.445 align:middle line:90%
so much.

00:33:03.445 --> 00:33:08.290 align:middle line:90%


00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:10.740 align:middle line:90%
OK, so there's a paper.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.240 align:middle line:84%
First paper I want to do is
Edelman, Ostrovsky, and Schwarz,

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:16.390 align:middle line:84%
which developed
the following model

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:21.490 align:middle line:84%
to think about how this Google
advertising system works

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:24.370 align:middle line:84%
and why it seems to
work so well for Google.

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:26.600 align:middle line:90%
So here's their model.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:28.600 align:middle line:90%
So there are n firms.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:30.800 align:middle line:90%
There will be m prizes.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:34.960 align:middle line:84%
So think of these positions
on the screen as the prizes.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:39.370 align:middle line:84%
And if you get prize
k, you get zk clicks.

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:41.320 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to
assume that z1 is bigger

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:42.880 align:middle line:90%
than z2 is bigger than zm.

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:48.220 align:middle line:84%
So just somehow exogenously,
slot one gets the most clicks,

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:51.135 align:middle line:84%
slot three gets third,
four, five, whatever.

00:33:51.135 --> 00:33:52.510 align:middle line:84%
So the slots are
ordered in terms

00:33:52.510 --> 00:33:55.133 align:middle line:84%
of number of times people
will click on them.

00:33:55.133 --> 00:33:56.800 align:middle line:84%
And it is true in
practice, people click

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:58.630 align:middle line:84%
on things on the top
much more than they

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:01.900 align:middle line:90%
click on things on the bottom.

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:05.620 align:middle line:84%
They're going to assume that
bidder i gets paid off zk times

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:10.409 align:middle line:84%
vi if that bidder
gets zk clicks.

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:14.120 align:middle line:84%
So as if each seller
has an expected profit

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:16.550 align:middle line:84%
they're going to get every
time someone clicks on their ad

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:19.909 align:middle line:84%
and their total payoff
is just number of clicks

00:34:19.909 --> 00:34:21.810 align:middle line:90%
times payoff per click.

00:34:21.810 --> 00:34:25.429 align:middle line:84%
And then it's the vi that's
the random variable that's

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:28.889 align:middle line:84%
known to bidder i and
unknown to Google.

00:34:28.889 --> 00:34:31.699 align:middle line:84%
And that's why you need the
bidding is to get the high vi

00:34:31.699 --> 00:34:34.250 align:middle line:90%
people to be displayed.

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:36.170 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that
advertising slots are

00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:38.730 align:middle line:84%
auctioned by the following
clock auction procedure.

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:41.489 align:middle line:84%
There's a price clock
that starts at 0.

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:46.310 align:middle line:84%
The price rises until there
are only m bidders left bidding

00:34:46.310 --> 00:34:48.060 align:middle line:90%
to try to be on the screen.

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:51.199 align:middle line:84%
And then from that point
on, every time a bidder

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:54.179 align:middle line:84%
drops out so that there are
only k minus 1 bidders left,

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:57.800 align:middle line:84%
the bidder who just
dropped out gets position k

00:34:57.800 --> 00:34:59.600 align:middle line:84%
and is assigned a per
click payment, which

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:01.460 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the drop out point
of the bidder in position

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:02.290 align:middle line:90%
k plus 1.

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:05.010 align:middle line:90%


00:35:05.010 --> 00:35:09.000 align:middle line:84%
So this is how they're going
to think about the Google

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.250 align:middle line:84%
mechanism of
submitting sealed bids

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:14.490 align:middle line:84%
with the k plus
first pricing plan

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.840 align:middle line:84%
as the outcome of
an English auction,

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:18.940 align:middle line:84%
just to specify the
game a little more.

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:22.560 align:middle line:84%
It turns out the game is better
specified as an English auction

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:23.820 align:middle line:90%
than as a sealed bid auction.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:25.528 align:middle line:84%
But they specify it
as an English auction

00:35:25.528 --> 00:35:28.650 align:middle line:84%
where just prices go up
and then every time--

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:30.270 align:middle line:90%
people drop out in order.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.540 align:middle line:84%
Once there are only 12 firms
left, 11th firm drops out,

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:34.530 align:middle line:90%
they get slot 11.

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:36.750 align:middle line:84%
10th firm drops out,
they get slot 10.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:40.110 align:middle line:84%
You pay the drop out point
of the person below you.

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:42.540 align:middle line:84%
So this is just
a way of modeling

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:45.620 align:middle line:84%
that sealed bid procedure
to give us a unique outcome.

00:35:45.620 --> 00:35:52.510 align:middle line:90%


00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:55.330 align:middle line:90%
Initial observations.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:58.330 align:middle line:84%
This generalized second
price auction mechanism

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:01.750 align:middle line:84%
that they're describing
is not the VCG mechanism.

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:03.430 align:middle line:84%
In the VCG mechanism,
you're having

00:36:03.430 --> 00:36:07.330 align:middle line:84%
to give bids over 50
factorial, over 39

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.420 align:middle line:84%
factorial possible
outcomes of the screen.

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:13.060 align:middle line:84%
Here you're just
submitting a single one

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:16.803 align:middle line:84%
dimensional real number, which
is just your pay per click.

00:36:16.803 --> 00:36:18.470 align:middle line:84%
So you're submitting
your pay per click.

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:20.530 align:middle line:90%
That's a very different thing.

00:36:20.530 --> 00:36:22.990 align:middle line:90%
So the message space is smaller.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:30.070 align:middle line:84%
And message space is smaller
and your payment is different.

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:33.340 align:middle line:84%
In the Google mechanism,
you pay the number

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:37.840 align:middle line:84%
of clicks you get times the
bid of the person below you.

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:41.080 align:middle line:90%
What's my VCG cost?

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:45.490 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose I take this
slot in the VCG mechanism.

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:47.500 align:middle line:84%
By taking this slot,
I move the person

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:50.320 align:middle line:84%
who would have been here down
to this slot and the person who

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:52.430 align:middle line:84%
would have been here down to
that slot, the person who would

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:55.097 align:middle line:84%
have been here to that slot, the
person who would have been here

00:36:55.097 --> 00:36:58.490 align:middle line:84%
to that slot, and this
person just gets nothing.

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:00.860 align:middle line:84%
So by taking this slot, I'm
doing a negative externality

00:37:00.860 --> 00:37:03.020 align:middle line:84%
on the person who would
have been here and here

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:04.850 align:middle line:90%
and here and here and here.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:09.350 align:middle line:84%
And so my VCG payment should
be the number of clicks

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:12.440 align:middle line:84%
that I take from somebody in
moving them from spot I down

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:14.017 align:middle line:90%
to spot i plus 1.

00:37:14.017 --> 00:37:16.100 align:middle line:84%
The number of clicks that
I take from someone else

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:18.653 align:middle line:84%
to move them from spot i
plus 1 down to spot i plus 2.

00:37:18.653 --> 00:37:20.570 align:middle line:84%
I multiply that by their
value, because that's

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:21.930 align:middle line:90%
how much I hurt them.

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:24.980 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, the person who
I bump off the screen entirely,

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:29.000 align:middle line:84%
they would have gotten zm clicks
and had payment vm plus 1.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:31.525 align:middle line:90%
That's how much I hurt them.

00:37:31.525 --> 00:37:35.150 align:middle line:90%


00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:37.740 align:middle line:90%
So it's not a VCG mechanism.

00:37:37.740 --> 00:37:39.960 align:middle line:84%
It just has a much
smaller bidding space.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't have the
negative externality payments.

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:46.460 align:middle line:84%
It has just the bidder
below you bid payment, not

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:49.172 align:middle line:84%
the weighted average of all
the bids of all the people

00:37:49.172 --> 00:37:50.005 align:middle line:90%
below your payments.

00:37:50.005 --> 00:37:52.520 align:middle line:90%


00:37:52.520 --> 00:37:55.423 align:middle line:84%
Truth telling is not
necessarily going

00:37:55.423 --> 00:37:56.840 align:middle line:84%
to be an equilibrium
when you have

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:00.010 align:middle line:84%
more than one bidder or more
than one slot available.

00:38:00.010 --> 00:38:03.440 align:middle line:84%
So I give this example
here of suppose there

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:06.200 align:middle line:90%
are two slots on the screen.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Slot one gives 200 clicks,
slot two gives 199 clicks,

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:12.170 align:middle line:90%
and there's no third slot.

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:15.290 align:middle line:84%
And then imagine you have three
bidders have these values.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:17.580 align:middle line:84%
High value bidder
has value of 10.

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:22.220 align:middle line:84%
Second has a value of four,
third has a value of two.

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:27.240 align:middle line:84%
With truth telling,
bidder one bids 10.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:29.520 align:middle line:90%
Bidder one gets the top slot.

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:33.810 align:middle line:84%
They get 200 clicks and they
get a profit of six per click,

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:36.180 align:middle line:84%
because they're paying four
for each click they get.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:39.810 align:middle line:90%
200 times 6 is 1,200.

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:44.550 align:middle line:84%
But if instead of
bidding 10 I bid three,

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.140 align:middle line:84%
then I move down to the
second slot on the screen.

00:38:47.140 --> 00:38:49.890 align:middle line:90%
I get 199 clicks instead of 200.

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:52.620 align:middle line:84%
But I'm only paying two for
them instead of paying four.

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:53.740 align:middle line:90%
So I wouldn't want to bid.

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:57.570 align:middle line:84%
Truthfully, here I would
drop my bid from 10

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:00.600 align:middle line:84%
and I would rather bid three,
because bidding three gets

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:04.920 align:middle line:84%
me almost the same number
of clicks at a lower price.

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:09.060 align:middle line:84%
So this Google
mechanism, it's not VCG.

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:11.340 align:middle line:84%
Different strategy space,
different payments, and it's

00:39:11.340 --> 00:39:13.590 align:middle line:84%
not VCG like in
that bidders-- it's

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:17.340 align:middle line:84%
going to be a non truth telling
equilibrium in this mechanism.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:18.840 align:middle line:84%
Kind of like the
first price auction

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.340 align:middle line:84%
is a non truth
telling mechanism.

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:22.530 align:middle line:84%
But bidders are going to
have to shade their bids,

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.700 align:middle line:84%
because bidding
truthfully isn't optimal.

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:32.880 align:middle line:90%


00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:33.935 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what happens?

00:39:33.935 --> 00:39:38.100 align:middle line:90%


00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:39.870 align:middle line:84%
Much like the first
price auction,

00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:41.370 align:middle line:84%
Edelman, Ostrovsky,
and Schwarz show

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:44.790 align:middle line:84%
that this model does have an
equilibrium in which bidders

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:46.440 align:middle line:90%
shade their bids.

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:51.570 align:middle line:84%
So if you're not going
to get on the screen,

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:54.290 align:middle line:84%
you always want to keep bidding
higher and higher if you

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:55.540 align:middle line:90%
need to, to get on the screen.

00:39:55.540 --> 00:39:58.330 align:middle line:84%
Because if you don't get on
the screen, you get nothing.

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:00.690 align:middle line:84%
So all the losing
bidders stay in

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:03.570 align:middle line:84%
until the clock reaches
their true value.

00:40:03.570 --> 00:40:08.700 align:middle line:84%
But then once you're
guaranteed a slot,

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:12.120 align:middle line:84%
when there are k bidders drop
out and the k plus first bid

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:17.460 align:middle line:84%
was bk plus 1, you drop out at
your true value minus something.

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:20.370 align:middle line:84%
And it's your value
minus something

00:40:20.370 --> 00:40:22.230 align:middle line:90%
that's a fraction of the--

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:24.738 align:middle line:84%
it's something related to the
surplus that you're getting

00:40:24.738 --> 00:40:27.030 align:middle line:84%
and the ratio of the number
of clicks of the two higher

00:40:27.030 --> 00:40:30.190 align:middle line:90%
slots, like the 200 over 199.

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:32.090 align:middle line:90%
199 over 200 thing.

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:36.383 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, it's your bid minus
some fraction of the surplus

00:40:36.383 --> 00:40:37.550 align:middle line:90%
that you're already getting.

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:39.090 align:middle line:90%
You drop out early.

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:42.585 align:middle line:84%
And the intuition
is you can't bid--

00:40:42.585 --> 00:40:46.630 align:middle line:90%


00:40:46.630 --> 00:40:50.140 align:middle line:84%
suppose I'm bidding against--
there are two of us.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:53.645 align:middle line:84%
I'm bidding to get on the
very top slot on the screen.

00:40:53.645 --> 00:40:55.270 align:middle line:84%
And there are two of
us who are hanging

00:40:55.270 --> 00:40:57.880 align:middle line:84%
in the bidding trying to
get the number one slot,

00:40:57.880 --> 00:41:00.790 align:middle line:90%
and my value is $10.

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:03.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, as the clock
is approaching $10,

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:05.860 align:middle line:84%
if my opponent hasn't
dropped out at $10

00:41:05.860 --> 00:41:07.540 align:middle line:84%
and I haven't
dropped out at $10,

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:09.998 align:middle line:84%
the worst thing on Earth for
me is if my opponent drops out

00:41:09.998 --> 00:41:11.050 align:middle line:90%
at $9.99.

00:41:11.050 --> 00:41:12.940 align:middle line:84%
Because if he
drops out at $9.99,

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:16.570 align:middle line:84%
I'm paying $9.99 per
click, and I'm only getting

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:18.020 align:middle line:90%
$10 a click worth of value.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:20.240 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm getting $0.01
per click in surplus.

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:23.270 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm getting
almost nothing.

00:41:23.270 --> 00:41:25.810 align:middle line:84%
So if someone else
dropped out at six,

00:41:25.810 --> 00:41:29.480 align:middle line:84%
there are two of us bidding
for the top two slots,

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:31.900 align:middle line:84%
at some point I want
to take my $4 per click

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:34.090 align:middle line:84%
for a lot of clicks in
the number two position

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:38.890 align:middle line:84%
rather than hang out and risk
the other guy dropping out.

00:41:38.890 --> 00:41:42.580 align:middle line:84%
Any time I'm mad if he drops
out, I shouldn't be there.

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:45.580 align:middle line:84%
And so there's going to be some
drop off point where you decide,

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:49.040 align:middle line:84%
like in a first price
auction, I want some surplus.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So I just drop out and take
my surplus in position two

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:56.710 align:middle line:84%
rather than risking this guy
drops out and leaving almost

00:41:56.710 --> 00:41:59.800 align:middle line:90%
no surplus in position one.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:03.806 align:middle line:84%
So there's going to be a
bid shading equilibrium.

00:42:03.806 --> 00:42:10.440 align:middle line:90%


00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:11.880 align:middle line:90%
Why is this?

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:18.180 align:middle line:84%
If you think about this
equilibrium drop out point

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:21.120 align:middle line:84%
b star of v. The
first order condition

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:24.570 align:middle line:84%
is I'm indifferent between
dropping out at b star of v

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:28.440 align:middle line:84%
and dropping out at b
star of v plus delta b.

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:32.070 align:middle line:84%
So what's the difference
in my payoff if I drop out

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:36.090 align:middle line:84%
at this one versus this one if
my opponent doesn't drop out

00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:37.028 align:middle line:90%
in between?

00:42:37.028 --> 00:42:38.820 align:middle line:84%
If my opponent doesn't
drop out in between,

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:43.080 align:middle line:84%
it makes no difference because
I'm still just paying the $6

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:46.017 align:middle line:90%
and getting slot number two.

00:42:46.017 --> 00:42:47.850 align:middle line:84%
So the only time it
makes a difference to me

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:52.710 align:middle line:84%
whether I stay in
from 857 to 858

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:57.390 align:middle line:84%
is if my opponent is going to
drop out between 857 and 858.

00:42:57.390 --> 00:43:00.210 align:middle line:84%
And so if this is the
optimum it must be,

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:03.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm indifferent between
dropping out at 857 and 858

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:06.270 align:middle line:84%
conditional on my
opponent dropping out

00:43:06.270 --> 00:43:08.280 align:middle line:84%
in between the
two of them, which

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:12.190 align:middle line:84%
would be that my payoff if I'm
the second person to drop out

00:43:12.190 --> 00:43:16.902 align:middle line:84%
is I get the higher slot
and I pay b star of v.

00:43:16.902 --> 00:43:18.610 align:middle line:84%
Essentially he's
dropping out essentially

00:43:18.610 --> 00:43:20.260 align:middle line:90%
where I was going to drop out.

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:23.140 align:middle line:84%
Or getting the lower slot
where I get fewer clicks,

00:43:23.140 --> 00:43:29.110 align:middle line:84%
zk instead of zk minus 1, but I
pay the $6 instead of the 857.

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:31.240 align:middle line:84%
And so anyway, so it's
this local indifference

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:34.210 align:middle line:84%
that tells me I'm locally
indifferent between dropping

00:43:34.210 --> 00:43:36.670 align:middle line:90%
out and getting the lower--

00:43:36.670 --> 00:43:38.170 align:middle line:84%
at the point where
I'm dropping out,

00:43:38.170 --> 00:43:40.503 align:middle line:84%
I'm locally indifferent between
dropping out and getting

00:43:40.503 --> 00:43:41.990 align:middle line:90%
the lower or the higher slot.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:43.750 align:middle line:84%
And that gives me an
expression that I can

00:43:43.750 --> 00:43:45.000 align:middle line:90%
use to solve for bid shading.

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:51.830 align:middle line:90%


00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:56.060 align:middle line:84%
And then sort of a corollary,
the bidding formula

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:57.150 align:middle line:90%
seemed unexciting.

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:58.530 align:middle line:90%
You shade your bid.

00:43:58.530 --> 00:44:02.000 align:middle line:84%
Remarkable corollary
is when players

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:04.640 align:middle line:84%
bid using those formulas
I just showed you,

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:07.040 align:middle line:84%
the Google auction
mechanism results

00:44:07.040 --> 00:44:10.340 align:middle line:84%
in every player making the same
payment schedule as they would

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:13.430 align:middle line:90%
have made in a VCG mechanism.

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:18.920 align:middle line:84%
So it's a non truth
telling equilibrium.

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:20.480 align:middle line:90%
Everyone shades their bids.

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:22.130 align:middle line:84%
But somehow in
equilibrium, everyone

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:24.050 align:middle line:84%
shades their bids in a
way that this exactly

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:26.965 align:middle line:90%
reproduces VCG payments.

00:44:26.965 --> 00:44:32.870 align:middle line:90%


00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:34.110 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:44:34.110 --> 00:44:38.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, just a simple
induction argument.

00:44:38.570 --> 00:44:40.260 align:middle line:90%
The losing bidders pay nothing.

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:43.220 align:middle line:84%
That's what happens
in the VCG mechanism.

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:45.380 align:middle line:84%
The bidder in the
very bottom position

00:44:45.380 --> 00:44:48.560 align:middle line:84%
on the screen who's bumping
someone else off the screen,

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:50.648 align:middle line:84%
they pay the number
of clicks they

00:44:50.648 --> 00:44:52.440 align:middle line:84%
get times the bid of
the person below them.

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:55.430 align:middle line:90%
That's also the VCG externality.

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:58.170 align:middle line:90%
And then just go one level up.

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:02.390 align:middle line:84%
What does the person
in this slot pay?

00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:04.340 align:middle line:84%
They pay the number
of clicks they get

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:06.840 align:middle line:90%
times the bid of this person.

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:08.870 align:middle line:84%
So they pay number of
clicks in position m

00:45:08.870 --> 00:45:13.550 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 times the bid of
the person in position m.

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:16.760 align:middle line:84%
But what was the bid of
the person in position m?

00:45:16.760 --> 00:45:20.330 align:middle line:84%
It was vm minus this
bid shading, which

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:25.400 align:middle line:84%
was zm over zm minus
1, vm minus bm plus 1.

00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:31.050 align:middle line:84%
When I multiply these things
together, I get zm minus 1vm.

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:35.220 align:middle line:84%
And then this cancels
and I get minus zm vm.

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:38.520 align:middle line:84%
So the first two terms
I get are the number

00:45:38.520 --> 00:45:42.630 align:middle line:84%
of clicks that I reduce,
the number of clicks

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:45.990 align:middle line:84%
that I cost the person who ends
up in position m multiplied

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:47.730 align:middle line:90%
by their value.

00:45:47.730 --> 00:45:49.350 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a
final term, which

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:50.950 align:middle line:90%
is this times this times this.

00:45:50.950 --> 00:45:53.580 align:middle line:90%
I get zm times vm plus 1.

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:55.590 align:middle line:84%
So I'm getting the
negative externality

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:59.580 align:middle line:84%
on the person m minus 1 plus
the negative externality

00:45:59.580 --> 00:46:01.080 align:middle line:90%
in person m.

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So somehow that
indifference condition

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:05.370 align:middle line:84%
that determines the
equilibrium bids

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:08.790 align:middle line:84%
results in the
equilibrium payment

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:10.740 align:middle line:84%
that I make being
the VCG payment.

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:13.473 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually paying that sum
of negative externalities

00:46:13.473 --> 00:46:15.140 align:middle line:84%
of all the people up
and down the table.

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:18.380 align:middle line:90%


00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:22.440 align:middle line:84%
And so do I have another
slide here, I take it?

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:25.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so implications.

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:28.430 align:middle line:84%
The fact that Google's
mechanism recreates VCG

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:30.820 align:middle line:84%
means that it's an
ex post equilibrium.

00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:33.090 align:middle line:84%
In VCG, you announce
your values.

00:46:33.090 --> 00:46:34.427 align:middle line:90%
It was a dominant strategy.

00:46:34.427 --> 00:46:36.260 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to change
what you announced.

00:46:36.260 --> 00:46:37.773 align:middle line:84%
In a first price
auction, you bid.

00:46:37.773 --> 00:46:39.440 align:middle line:84%
You see that you've
been slightly outbid

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:40.520 align:middle line:90%
when you bid v over 2.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:42.350 align:middle line:90%
You want to change your bid.

00:46:42.350 --> 00:46:45.290 align:middle line:84%
In a VCG mechanism, you
make your announcement.

00:46:45.290 --> 00:46:46.260 align:middle line:90%
You make your payment.

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:48.052 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to change
your announcement,

00:46:48.052 --> 00:46:51.080 align:middle line:84%
because your announcement
was a dominant strategy.

00:46:51.080 --> 00:46:53.420 align:middle line:84%
The fact that this
is recreating VCG

00:46:53.420 --> 00:46:56.420 align:middle line:84%
means you don't want to
change your bid when you've

00:46:56.420 --> 00:46:58.110 align:middle line:90%
seen what the other people bid.

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:01.222 align:middle line:84%
You're making the VCG payments
given your true value, assuming

00:47:01.222 --> 00:47:02.930 align:middle line:84%
everyone else announces
their true value,

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:05.870 align:middle line:84%
everyone plays the equilibrium
given their true value.

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:09.170 align:middle line:84%
You're making your VCG payment
given the announcements

00:47:09.170 --> 00:47:10.560 align:middle line:90%
that are being made.

00:47:10.560 --> 00:47:13.290 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, it's an
ex post equilibrium.

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:15.270 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to
change your bid.

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:20.220 align:middle line:84%
And computer science wise,
this is an incredibly important

00:47:20.220 --> 00:47:22.200 align:middle line:84%
aspect to making a
system work efficiently,

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:24.090 align:middle line:84%
is you don't want
everyone to have

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:27.780 align:middle line:84%
to have a bot that's repricing
their bids every millisecond,

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:29.400 align:middle line:84%
seeing what everyone
else is doing.

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:33.700 align:middle line:84%
By having the VCG payments,
it's an ex post equilibrium.

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:35.770 align:middle line:84%
You're happy to have
your bids stay the same.

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:37.770 align:middle line:84%
Everyone can just put in
these equilibrium bids

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:39.330 align:middle line:90%
and then let them sit there.

00:47:39.330 --> 00:47:41.340 align:middle line:84%
And it's not like I'm
disappointed that I'm

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:42.550 align:middle line:90%
third instead of second.

00:47:42.550 --> 00:47:45.850 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I put in my bid
and I'm happy where I am.

00:47:45.850 --> 00:47:48.827 align:middle line:84%
If I had ended up third instead
of second, that would be good.

00:47:48.827 --> 00:47:51.410 align:middle line:84%
But given what other people are
bidding, I don't want to move.

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:55.510 align:middle line:90%


00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:57.580 align:middle line:84%
Another implication
of VCG equivalence

00:47:57.580 --> 00:48:00.400 align:middle line:84%
is that this mechanism
is efficient.

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:02.830 align:middle line:84%
The winners are the firms
that derive the most

00:48:02.830 --> 00:48:05.770 align:middle line:90%
value from being listed.

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:08.170 align:middle line:84%
And this gives you
a suggestion for why

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:13.600 align:middle line:84%
it may be that the Google
ad pages work so well.

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:20.770 align:middle line:84%
If it's the case that
value of an ad for shorts,

00:48:20.770 --> 00:48:23.200 align:middle line:84%
the profit that
goes to the seller

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:26.470 align:middle line:84%
is proportional to
the consumer surplus.

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:28.840 align:middle line:84%
Because consumer
surplus tends to be

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:32.260 align:middle line:84%
divided evenly between sellers
and buyers in an equilibrium.

00:48:32.260 --> 00:48:37.060 align:middle line:84%
Then Google is picking out
the highest value advertisers

00:48:37.060 --> 00:48:38.510 align:middle line:90%
there via this mechanism.

00:48:38.510 --> 00:48:42.820 align:middle line:84%
Whatever this page max, over
all possible 50 factorial

00:48:42.820 --> 00:48:44.980 align:middle line:84%
over 39 factorial
orientations, this

00:48:44.980 --> 00:48:49.570 align:middle line:84%
is picking out the page that
gives the largest sum of values

00:48:49.570 --> 00:48:51.820 align:middle line:84%
to the firms, which may be
the largest sum of consumer

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:53.330 align:middle line:90%
surpluses to the consumers.

00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:56.960 align:middle line:90%


00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:01.490 align:middle line:84%
Third observation is remember I
said VCG is not budget balanced.

00:49:01.490 --> 00:49:04.780 align:middle line:84%
This is what I sometimes call
the commercial genius of Google.

00:49:04.780 --> 00:49:08.600 align:middle line:84%
That is, VCG is not
budget balanced.

00:49:08.600 --> 00:49:10.940 align:middle line:84%
The only way to
choose the right ads

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:12.920 align:middle line:84%
is to have firms
making VCG payments

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:14.660 align:middle line:90%
for being on the screen.

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:16.490 align:middle line:90%
Where's all that money flowing?

00:49:16.490 --> 00:49:19.490 align:middle line:84%
It's flowing from the
advertisers to Google.

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:21.260 align:middle line:84%
And let's suppose
Bing were to say,

00:49:21.260 --> 00:49:23.510 align:middle line:84%
OK, we would like
you to-- we're going

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:25.970 align:middle line:84%
to try to steal advertisers
away from Google

00:49:25.970 --> 00:49:29.090 align:middle line:84%
by charging the advertisers
less and hoping the advertisers

00:49:29.090 --> 00:49:32.000 align:middle line:84%
will abandon Google and
advertise on Bing instead.

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:34.590 align:middle line:84%
This theorem basically
says you can't do that.

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:37.700 align:middle line:84%
You can't charge the
advertisers any less than this

00:49:37.700 --> 00:49:40.440 align:middle line:84%
and find out which are
the best ads to show.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:42.860 align:middle line:84%
So Google, in some
sense, has this immunity

00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:45.950 align:middle line:84%
to undercutting in price
that many firms don't have.

00:49:45.950 --> 00:49:48.460 align:middle line:84%
The theorem is if you try
to undercut Google on price,

00:49:48.460 --> 00:49:50.210 align:middle line:84%
you're picking the
wrong ads and consumers

00:49:50.210 --> 00:49:51.760 align:middle line:90%
will like the page less.

00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:57.690 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
to do this business

00:49:57.690 --> 00:50:00.810 align:middle line:84%
and to choose the right ads, you
have to charge the advertisers

00:50:00.810 --> 00:50:01.970 align:middle line:90%
this much.

00:50:01.970 --> 00:50:05.100 align:middle line:84%
And price competition
doesn't work the way

00:50:05.100 --> 00:50:06.110 align:middle line:90%
it would otherwise work.

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:16.080 align:middle line:90%


00:50:16.080 --> 00:50:19.110 align:middle line:84%
At one point, Microsoft had,
maybe they still have it,

00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:21.120 align:middle line:90%
they had Bing Rewards.

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:23.488 align:middle line:84%
They had a big rewards program
for the advertisers where

00:50:23.488 --> 00:50:25.530 align:middle line:84%
it would be like if you
advertised a lot on Bing,

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:27.265 align:middle line:84%
we'll give you an
advertising credit

00:50:27.265 --> 00:50:29.140 align:middle line:84%
and give you 50% back
at the end of the month

00:50:29.140 --> 00:50:31.320 align:middle line:84%
for having bought
a lot of Bing ads.

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:32.890 align:middle line:84%
What's the equivalent
of that game?

00:50:32.890 --> 00:50:35.378 align:middle line:84%
Just everyone bids twice
as much, get 50% back,

00:50:35.378 --> 00:50:37.170 align:middle line:84%
and then they're happy
with their position.

00:50:37.170 --> 00:50:40.050 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, it's
hard to undo this logic

00:50:40.050 --> 00:50:43.530 align:middle line:84%
that if you want to
choose the right things,

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:45.590 align:middle line:84%
firms have to make
the VCG payments.

00:50:45.590 --> 00:50:46.880 align:middle line:90%
It's hard to mess with that.

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:54.176 align:middle line:90%


00:50:54.176 --> 00:50:55.790 align:middle line:90%
So other issues.

00:50:55.790 --> 00:50:59.240 align:middle line:84%
I think Edelman, Ostrovsky,
Schwarz is a beautiful theorem.

00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:03.260 align:middle line:84%
I think in practice,
these weights

00:51:03.260 --> 00:51:05.720 align:middle line:90%
are incredibly important.

00:51:05.720 --> 00:51:11.420 align:middle line:84%
The weights are what let you
do this pay per click auction.

00:51:11.420 --> 00:51:14.990 align:middle line:84%
Because without the weights,
you couldn't do pay per click

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:18.470 align:middle line:84%
and have the right bidders
selected, because then there'd

00:51:18.470 --> 00:51:22.130 align:middle line:84%
be this big bias towards people
whose ads don't get clicked on

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:25.580 align:middle line:84%
or people don't get clicked
on unless for people who--

00:51:25.580 --> 00:51:27.900 align:middle line:84%
most people in search are
not interested in those ads.

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:30.650 align:middle line:84%
And so the weights are
incredibly important.

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:31.920 align:middle line:90%
The weights do reflect--

00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:33.920 align:middle line:84%
if you look at the weights
that Google is using,

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:36.780 align:middle line:84%
the weights clearly reflect what
people are going to click on.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:38.720 align:middle line:84%
But the weights also
reflect much more

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:39.900 align:middle line:84%
than what people are
going to click on.

00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:41.317 align:middle line:84%
The weights are
also going to have

00:51:41.317 --> 00:51:43.820 align:middle line:84%
to reflect whether people buy
for something after clicking

00:51:43.820 --> 00:51:45.403 align:middle line:84%
on it rather than
they're disappointed

00:51:45.403 --> 00:51:46.765 align:middle line:90%
after clicking on it and so on.

00:51:46.765 --> 00:51:49.460 align:middle line:90%


00:51:49.460 --> 00:51:53.520 align:middle line:84%
Google makes liberal use
of reservation prices.

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:56.790 align:middle line:84%
Many, many pages have
no ads whatsoever.

00:51:56.790 --> 00:51:59.850 align:middle line:84%
That Math Counts
page, I've got a book

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:04.300 align:middle line:84%
to sell that has middle
school math practice for kids.

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:08.370 align:middle line:84%
If I'm not willing to
bid $3 or $2 a click,

00:52:08.370 --> 00:52:10.290 align:middle line:84%
Google is not going
to display it.

00:52:10.290 --> 00:52:12.927 align:middle line:84%
And they've chosen a very high
reservation price for that page.

00:52:12.927 --> 00:52:14.760 align:middle line:84%
They're not going to
show ads that have less

00:52:14.760 --> 00:52:17.760 align:middle line:90%
than that reservation price.

00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:20.122 align:middle line:84%
I think Edelman,
Ostrovsky, Schwarz is not

00:52:20.122 --> 00:52:22.080 align:middle line:84%
an adequate tool for
thinking about the welfare

00:52:22.080 --> 00:52:23.400 align:middle line:90%
implications of that.

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:28.170 align:middle line:84%
In Edelman, Ostrovsky,
Schwarz, reservation prices

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:31.200 align:middle line:84%
are purely consumer
welfare reducing.

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.340 align:middle line:84%
If these firms have values
for all of these slots,

00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:37.442 align:middle line:84%
then by not putting
the ad on there,

00:52:37.442 --> 00:52:39.150 align:middle line:84%
you're just reducing
the number of people

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:41.700 align:middle line:90%
who click on them, the total.

00:52:41.700 --> 00:52:45.450 align:middle line:84%
You can't get any consumer
surplus if there's no click.

00:52:45.450 --> 00:52:49.470 align:middle line:84%
So Edelman, Ostrovsky,
Schwarz would say the fact

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:51.780 align:middle line:84%
that Google is using
reserve prices is evidence

00:52:51.780 --> 00:52:55.710 align:middle line:84%
that it's exerting market power
and market power is always bad.

00:52:55.710 --> 00:52:58.950 align:middle line:84%
So I think the next
paper I'm going to cover

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:01.583 align:middle line:84%
is about, in part, how do we
think about reserve prices

00:53:01.583 --> 00:53:02.750 align:middle line:90%
and why they may not be bad.

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:06.350 align:middle line:90%


00:53:06.350 --> 00:53:08.930 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, so next paper.

00:53:08.930 --> 00:53:11.260 align:middle line:84%
Any questions on that
before I go on to mine?

00:53:11.260 --> 00:53:16.790 align:middle line:90%


00:53:16.790 --> 00:53:20.420 align:middle line:84%
Athey-Ellison, position
auctions with consumer search.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:23.480 align:middle line:84%
The main idea of our paper
is to endogenize the prizes

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:26.150 align:middle line:84%
in the Edelman Ostrovsky,
Schwarz model as the profits

00:53:26.150 --> 00:53:28.740 align:middle line:84%
that you get from selling
to consumer population.

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:30.590 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is going
to be that we're

00:53:30.590 --> 00:53:33.740 align:middle line:84%
going to marry Edelman,
Ostrovsky, Schwarz with a search

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:38.000 align:middle line:84%
model where consumers have
search costs that they incur

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:40.672 align:middle line:84%
every time they click on an
ad and only click on an ad

00:53:40.672 --> 00:53:43.130 align:middle line:84%
if they think that the ad is
going to have something that's

00:53:43.130 --> 00:53:44.065 align:middle line:90%
worth showing them.

00:53:44.065 --> 00:53:46.700 align:middle line:90%


00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:48.660 align:middle line:84%
What are motivations
for doing this?

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:51.380 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, thinking about
the consumers clicking

00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:52.953 align:middle line:90%
seems like a reasonable channel.

00:53:52.953 --> 00:53:55.850 align:middle line:90%


00:53:55.850 --> 00:53:57.713 align:middle line:84%
Think about where
the value comes from

00:53:57.713 --> 00:53:59.880 align:middle line:84%
is going to think about
changes you're going to want

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:01.400 align:middle line:90%
to make to the standard model.

00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:07.500 align:middle line:84%
In particular, one idea we have
is that reserve prices need not

00:54:07.500 --> 00:54:09.720 align:middle line:90%
be welfare reducing.

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:13.693 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, why are reserve
prices not welfare reducing?

00:54:13.693 --> 00:54:15.610 align:middle line:84%
Consumers have to always
make these decisions.

00:54:15.610 --> 00:54:17.110 align:middle line:90%
Do I click on ads?

00:54:17.110 --> 00:54:19.190 align:middle line:90%
If every page you put on--

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:21.690 align:middle line:84%
if you're Yahoo! and every page
you put up there, it's like,

00:54:21.690 --> 00:54:24.130 align:middle line:84%
here are the Paris
Hilton ringtones thing,

00:54:24.130 --> 00:54:25.390 align:middle line:90%
what do consumers learn?

00:54:25.390 --> 00:54:27.765 align:middle line:84%
Consumers learn that the upper
right corner of the screen

00:54:27.765 --> 00:54:30.720 align:middle line:84%
is where you can find
the find x ringtones ad.

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:32.800 align:middle line:90%
Consumers won't even read that.

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:33.700 align:middle line:90%
They won't read it.

00:54:33.700 --> 00:54:34.750 align:middle line:90%
They will never click on it.

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:37.110 align:middle line:84%
If there actually is something
that's useful to them up there,

00:54:37.110 --> 00:54:37.900 align:middle line:90%
they won't see it.

00:54:37.900 --> 00:54:39.580 align:middle line:84%
They won't consider
clicking on it.

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:42.180 align:middle line:84%
So by using a reserve
price, reserve prices

00:54:42.180 --> 00:54:45.810 align:middle line:84%
can convince consumers that
there is probably something.

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:47.283 align:middle line:84%
The fact that when
you see an ad,

00:54:47.283 --> 00:54:49.950 align:middle line:84%
you know that person was willing
to pay the reserve price, which

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:52.230 align:middle line:84%
means they probably have
something good to sell you.

00:54:52.230 --> 00:54:53.880 align:middle line:84%
So you should read
it and you should

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:55.470 align:middle line:84%
think about clicking
on it, depending

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:57.210 align:middle line:90%
on your clicking costs.

00:54:57.210 --> 00:55:00.300 align:middle line:84%
But the idea is that reserve
prices can be valuable as a way

00:55:00.300 --> 00:55:03.420 align:middle line:84%
to signal to consumers
that the ads that there

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:05.370 align:middle line:84%
are worth clicking on
and then can actually

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:06.990 align:middle line:84%
increase the total
number of clicks

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:09.720 align:middle line:84%
rather than always decreasing
the total number of clicks.

00:55:09.720 --> 00:55:12.330 align:middle line:84%
And then also just
we want to think

00:55:12.330 --> 00:55:14.610 align:middle line:84%
about when you have a
model with the consumers,

00:55:14.610 --> 00:55:17.340 align:middle line:84%
you can't think about consumer
and social welfare in Edelman,

00:55:17.340 --> 00:55:21.840 align:middle line:84%
Ostrovsky, Schwarz because the
consumers are just mindlessly

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:23.053 align:middle line:90%
clicking and buying things.

00:55:23.053 --> 00:55:24.720 align:middle line:84%
You don't have the
consumer search costs

00:55:24.720 --> 00:55:27.387 align:middle line:84%
that are being incurred when you
put the ads on the screen, what

00:55:27.387 --> 00:55:31.630 align:middle line:84%
you think about what the
consumer welfare is going to be.

00:55:31.630 --> 00:55:34.660 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, our model
basic elements.

00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:36.250 align:middle line:84%
Consumers are going
to have a need.

00:55:36.250 --> 00:55:38.060 align:middle line:84%
A need could be
a pair of shorts.

00:55:38.060 --> 00:55:43.480 align:middle line:84%
They can meet this need by
purchasing from some advertiser.

00:55:43.480 --> 00:55:45.280 align:middle line:84%
Consumers incur
some kind of search

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:47.740 align:middle line:84%
costs every time
they click on a link.

00:55:47.740 --> 00:55:50.180 align:middle line:84%
The advertisers
differ in quality.

00:55:50.180 --> 00:55:52.990 align:middle line:84%
Quality is just going to be the
probability of having something

00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:54.340 align:middle line:90%
the consumer wants to buy.

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.693 align:middle line:84%
So everyone has some pair
of shorts they want to buy.

00:55:57.693 --> 00:55:59.110 align:middle line:84%
If you click on
their ad, they may

00:55:59.110 --> 00:56:03.160 align:middle line:84%
have the pair of shorts you
want, in which case you buy it.

00:56:03.160 --> 00:56:06.550 align:middle line:84%
Quality is private information
of the advertisers.

00:56:06.550 --> 00:56:08.680 align:middle line:84%
Some advertisers know that
90% of people who click

00:56:08.680 --> 00:56:10.010 align:middle line:90%
will find the shorts they want.

00:56:10.010 --> 00:56:13.420 align:middle line:84%
30% will find it,
10% will find it.

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:18.820 align:middle line:84%
And then if the bids do end up
being ordered in a way that's

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:21.743 align:middle line:84%
monotone in quality, like the
highest quality firm is here,

00:56:21.743 --> 00:56:24.160 align:middle line:84%
the second highest quality
firm is here, the third highest

00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:29.380 align:middle line:84%
quality firm is here, then that
creates guided consumer search

00:56:29.380 --> 00:56:31.130 align:middle line:84%
where instead of having
to search randomly

00:56:31.130 --> 00:56:34.010 align:middle line:84%
over all the firms, you know
to click on the highest quality

00:56:34.010 --> 00:56:36.260 align:middle line:84%
firm first, then the second
highest quality firm, then

00:56:36.260 --> 00:56:37.730 align:middle line:90%
the third highest quality firm.

00:56:37.730 --> 00:56:40.400 align:middle line:84%
And that guiding in your
search can be, in some sense,

00:56:40.400 --> 00:56:44.060 align:middle line:84%
it's a intermediary that
improves the quality of search

00:56:44.060 --> 00:56:47.640 align:middle line:84%
by giving you partial
information about the qualities.

00:56:47.640 --> 00:56:53.390 align:middle line:90%


00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:59.770 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, observations
in the paper.

00:56:59.770 --> 00:57:02.270 align:middle line:84%
You can think about
these sponsored searches

00:57:02.270 --> 00:57:05.960 align:middle line:84%
as a means of as an information
intermediary providing

00:57:05.960 --> 00:57:07.490 align:middle line:84%
partial information
to consumers,

00:57:07.490 --> 00:57:10.910 align:middle line:84%
which is this ordering
of the firms on quality.

00:57:10.910 --> 00:57:13.700 align:middle line:84%
We can generalize some of the
Edelman, Ostrovsky, Schwarz

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:18.380 align:middle line:84%
model to this, even though there
are common value elements now.

00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:21.620 align:middle line:84%
Reserve prices can
improve social welfare.

00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:25.160 align:middle line:84%
And the standard results about
click weighting being weighted.

00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:26.410 align:middle line:90%
Things I'll discuss.

00:57:26.410 --> 00:57:28.732 align:middle line:84%
You would want to do the
weighting differently

00:57:28.732 --> 00:57:30.440 align:middle line:84%
in this model than
you would do weighting

00:57:30.440 --> 00:57:32.330 align:middle line:90%
in Edelman, Ostrovsky, Schwarz.

00:57:32.330 --> 00:57:35.310 align:middle line:84%
I'll do this relatively quickly
and skip some of the results.

00:57:35.310 --> 00:57:38.510 align:middle line:84%
But so the basic model is we
have this continuum of consumers

00:57:38.510 --> 00:57:40.760 align:middle line:90%
of unit mass.

00:57:40.760 --> 00:57:42.740 align:middle line:90%
Everybody has a need.

00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:46.040 align:middle line:84%
They get a payoff of 1
if they meet their need.

00:57:46.040 --> 00:57:50.113 align:middle line:84%
The consumers have search costs
sj from clicking on a link.

00:57:50.113 --> 00:57:52.280 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to
assume that search costs are

00:57:52.280 --> 00:57:54.360 align:middle line:90%
heterogeneous in the population.

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:56.580 align:middle line:84%
And what consumers do
is they search optimally

00:57:56.580 --> 00:57:59.280 align:middle line:84%
until their need is met or
the benefit of further search

00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:02.970 align:middle line:90%
falls below sj.

00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:06.510 align:middle line:84%
We have n advertisers bidding
to be the sponsored links.

00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:10.110 align:middle line:84%
Firm I has probability qi of
meeting a random consumer's

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:10.920 align:middle line:90%
need.

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:13.830 align:middle line:84%
qi is private
information of the firms.

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:17.040 align:middle line:84%
Firms get a pay off of
1 if they meet a need.

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:18.900 align:middle line:84%
And what the search
engine is going to do

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:22.080 align:middle line:84%
is conduct the standard,
unweighted, generalized second

00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:26.190 align:middle line:84%
price auction, like in
Edelman, Ostrovsky, Schwarz.

00:58:26.190 --> 00:58:28.740 align:middle line:84%
And then they display
m sponsored links

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:30.185 align:middle line:90%
ordered according to the bids.

00:58:30.185 --> 00:58:36.060 align:middle line:90%


00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:40.050 align:middle line:84%
So just benchmark for how the
improvement in search quality

00:58:40.050 --> 00:58:42.450 align:middle line:90%
works.

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:45.003 align:middle line:84%
What would you do if
all you saw is the ads

00:58:45.003 --> 00:58:47.170 align:middle line:84%
and the ads had no ordering
to them, what do you do?

00:58:47.170 --> 00:58:49.140 align:middle line:90%
You just click on a random ad.

00:58:49.140 --> 00:58:52.240 align:middle line:84%
You have some expectation of
the quality of a random ad.

00:58:52.240 --> 00:58:54.720 align:middle line:84%
I either don't click on anything
or I click one random ad,

00:58:54.720 --> 00:58:57.380 align:middle line:84%
and then I just keep
clicking random ads.

00:58:57.380 --> 00:58:59.130 align:middle line:84%
Every ad is independent
of every other ad,

00:58:59.130 --> 00:59:00.660 align:middle line:84%
so I just keep clicking
random ads to see if they have

00:59:00.660 --> 00:59:02.300 align:middle line:84%
what I want until
I run out of ads.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:09.060 align:middle line:90%


00:59:09.060 --> 00:59:11.800 align:middle line:84%
What do you do if
the ads are ordered?

00:59:11.800 --> 00:59:14.100 align:middle line:84%
If the ads are sorted by
quality and equilibrium,

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:17.520 align:middle line:84%
then consumers follow
a top down strategy.

00:59:17.520 --> 00:59:19.290 align:middle line:84%
They start at the
top and they just

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:22.680 align:middle line:84%
continue clicking until
either their need is met

00:59:22.680 --> 00:59:25.530 align:middle line:84%
or until the expected
quality of the next website

00:59:25.530 --> 00:59:27.400 align:middle line:90%
is less than the search cost.

00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And as you move down the
screen, expected quality

00:59:31.200 --> 00:59:33.190 align:middle line:90%
is decreasing for two reasons.

00:59:33.190 --> 00:59:39.840 align:middle line:84%
One is, you know that the
firms were ordered on quality.

00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:41.820 align:middle line:84%
So that as you're
going further down,

00:59:41.820 --> 00:59:45.090 align:middle line:84%
you're getting a lower order
statistic from the distribution.

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:47.370 align:middle line:84%
And then you're also Bayesian
updating, because you're

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:50.280 align:middle line:84%
learning that if the top three
firms didn't have what I'm

00:59:50.280 --> 00:59:53.250 align:middle line:84%
looking for, it must
be that the set of ads

00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:56.310 align:middle line:84%
is lower quality than I
thought it was ex ante,

00:59:56.310 --> 00:59:58.448 align:middle line:84%
and the realizations
were all low.

00:59:58.448 --> 01:00:00.240 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the realization
of the fifth one

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:02.740 align:middle line:84%
is even lower than my ex ante
expectation of the realization

01:00:02.740 --> 01:00:03.670 align:middle line:90%
of the fifth one.

01:00:03.670 --> 01:00:07.045 align:middle line:84%
So as you move down the
list, your expectation

01:00:07.045 --> 01:00:08.670 align:middle line:84%
of the probability
of meeting your need

01:00:08.670 --> 01:00:09.850 align:middle line:90%
goes down for two reasons.

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:11.610 align:middle line:84%
You know they're a
lower order statistic

01:00:11.610 --> 01:00:13.920 align:middle line:84%
and you know that the top
firms were no good, which

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:15.712 align:middle line:84%
makes you think that
the whole set of draws

01:00:15.712 --> 01:00:17.580 align:middle line:84%
from the distribution
were a low draw.

01:00:17.580 --> 01:00:20.280 align:middle line:84%
And so as you move down, you're
just going to start at the top

01:00:20.280 --> 01:00:24.510 align:middle line:84%
and then move down until your
updated expectation, given

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:28.320 align:middle line:84%
that you've had a whole bunch
of failures to meet your need

01:00:28.320 --> 01:00:32.100 align:middle line:90%
is less than your search cost.

01:00:32.100 --> 01:00:33.800 align:middle line:84%
What happens in
the bidding game?

01:00:33.800 --> 01:00:38.670 align:middle line:90%


01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:41.390 align:middle line:84%
Bidding game is just like
Edelman, Ostrovsky, Schwarz.

01:00:41.390 --> 01:00:44.060 align:middle line:90%


01:00:44.060 --> 01:00:46.500 align:middle line:84%
Suppose firms dropped
out at these prices.

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:49.967 align:middle line:84%
You're deciding how long
to stay and accept slot k.

01:00:49.967 --> 01:00:52.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, you're just going
to have this indifference

01:00:52.050 --> 01:00:54.780 align:middle line:84%
between dropping
out at this b star

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:56.490 align:middle line:84%
and dropping out
at b star plus db.

01:00:56.490 --> 01:00:59.460 align:middle line:90%


01:00:59.460 --> 01:01:07.560 align:middle line:84%
If you're in the slot k minus
1, if you're in the higher slot,

01:01:07.560 --> 01:01:10.590 align:middle line:84%
you get qi minus bk
times the number of slots

01:01:10.590 --> 01:01:13.650 align:middle line:90%
that go to slot k minus 1.

01:01:13.650 --> 01:01:15.900 align:middle line:90%
But what happens if you drop--

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:18.220 align:middle line:84%
I'm bidding against another
firm for slot number one.

01:01:18.220 --> 01:01:21.490 align:middle line:84%
What happens if I
take the lower thing?

01:01:21.490 --> 01:01:23.850 align:middle line:84%
If I take the lower
slot, I get q minus bk

01:01:23.850 --> 01:01:28.830 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 times my demand
for being in slot k.

01:01:28.830 --> 01:01:30.960 align:middle line:84%
But my demand for
being in slot k

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:33.100 align:middle line:84%
depends on the quality
of all the firms

01:01:33.100 --> 01:01:34.730 align:middle line:90%
that are in higher slots.

01:01:34.730 --> 01:01:36.130 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm bidding
against someone

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:38.020 align:middle line:84%
and I realize that
they have quality one,

01:01:38.020 --> 01:01:39.437 align:middle line:84%
then I don't want
to lose to them.

01:01:39.437 --> 01:01:41.320 align:middle line:84%
Because if I lose
to them, a consumer

01:01:41.320 --> 01:01:43.000 align:middle line:90%
is never going to buy from me.

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.730 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, it's a
common value auction instead

01:01:45.730 --> 01:01:48.070 align:middle line:84%
of a private value
auction, because I

01:01:48.070 --> 01:01:50.690 align:middle line:84%
care about the quality of the
firms I'm bidding against.

01:01:50.690 --> 01:01:53.800 align:middle line:84%
Because if they're really
high quality and I get

01:01:53.800 --> 01:01:56.133 align:middle line:84%
outbid by them, I'm never
going to make a sale.

01:01:56.133 --> 01:01:57.550 align:middle line:84%
And if they're
really low quality,

01:01:57.550 --> 01:02:00.093 align:middle line:84%
I don't care about being outbid
by them, because they're not

01:02:00.093 --> 01:02:02.260 align:middle line:84%
going to make the sale
anyway, so I might as well be

01:02:02.260 --> 01:02:03.530 align:middle line:90%
in the second position.

01:02:03.530 --> 01:02:06.460 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about it, if
we had the rare draw where

01:02:06.460 --> 01:02:15.550 align:middle line:84%
all the qualities
were almost 0, there's

01:02:15.550 --> 01:02:17.800 align:middle line:84%
no reason to bid for the
second versus the first slot,

01:02:17.800 --> 01:02:18.850 align:middle line:84%
because the consumers
are going to think

01:02:18.850 --> 01:02:20.365 align:middle line:90%
the second firm is really good.

01:02:20.365 --> 01:02:22.990 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well just take the
second slot with the first guy

01:02:22.990 --> 01:02:24.850 align:middle line:90%
have 0 quality and outbid me.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:27.900 align:middle line:90%
I'm fine.

01:02:27.900 --> 01:02:32.950 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that despite this
being a common value auction,

01:02:32.950 --> 01:02:34.910 align:middle line:90%
the same argument goes through.

01:02:34.910 --> 01:02:38.360 align:middle line:84%
You can characterize what
are the equilibrium bids.

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:40.720 align:middle line:84%
It's easy to find
the equilibrium bids.

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:45.940 align:middle line:84%
So model still has a
solution like the other model

01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:47.830 align:middle line:90%
where there's some bid shading.

01:02:47.830 --> 01:02:48.640 align:middle line:90%
You shade your bid.

01:02:48.640 --> 01:02:51.460 align:middle line:84%
You shade your
bid because you're

01:02:51.460 --> 01:02:53.260 align:middle line:84%
going to get fewer
clicks for two reasons.

01:02:53.260 --> 01:02:57.308 align:middle line:84%
Because the posterior
is getting worse

01:02:57.308 --> 01:02:59.600 align:middle line:84%
and because they might have
made the sale ahead of you.

01:02:59.600 --> 01:03:00.700 align:middle line:84%
So you have the two
different factors

01:03:00.700 --> 01:03:01.950 align:middle line:90%
leading you to shade your bid.

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:05.265 align:middle line:90%
But it's same deal.

01:03:05.265 --> 01:03:08.980 align:middle line:90%


01:03:08.980 --> 01:03:13.000 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, but again, I think
the heart of this paper,

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:14.880 align:middle line:84%
like Edelman,
Ostrovsky, Schwarz was

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:16.540 align:middle line:84%
the heart of the
paper was a theorem

01:03:16.540 --> 01:03:19.360 align:middle line:90%
about this reproduces VCG.

01:03:19.360 --> 01:03:24.040 align:middle line:84%
Ours, we have this theorem
about welfare effects

01:03:24.040 --> 01:03:25.520 align:middle line:90%
of reserve prices.

01:03:25.520 --> 01:03:28.550 align:middle line:84%
And so here's a theorem
on reserve prices.

01:03:28.550 --> 01:03:30.830 align:middle line:84%
Suppose search costs are
uniformly distributed.

01:03:30.830 --> 01:03:35.020 align:middle line:84%
An important property
is that consumer surplus

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:39.490 align:middle line:84%
and social welfare are maximized
at exactly the same reserve

01:03:39.490 --> 01:03:40.510 align:middle line:90%
price.

01:03:40.510 --> 01:03:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, given
any bidding behavior

01:03:42.550 --> 01:03:45.000 align:middle line:84%
by advertiser and any
reserve price policy,

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:48.970 align:middle line:84%
the search engine equilibrium
behavior by consumers

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:52.540 align:middle line:84%
implies that the expected
social welfare is just three

01:03:52.540 --> 01:03:56.150 align:middle line:84%
times the expected surplus
or expected advertiser

01:03:56.150 --> 01:03:59.430 align:middle line:84%
and Google surplus is always
twice expected consumer surplus.

01:03:59.430 --> 01:04:03.800 align:middle line:84%
So there's this social optimum
that coincides with the consumer

01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:05.330 align:middle line:90%
optimum.

01:04:05.330 --> 01:04:08.575 align:middle line:84%
And the proof of that
is pretty simple.

01:04:08.575 --> 01:04:10.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to find this
object called gross consumer

01:04:10.700 --> 01:04:14.210 align:middle line:84%
surplus, which is consumer
surplus plus the search

01:04:14.210 --> 01:04:16.010 align:middle line:90%
costs incurred.

01:04:16.010 --> 01:04:18.050 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to find
gross producer surplus

01:04:18.050 --> 01:04:20.570 align:middle line:84%
to be the advertiser
profit plus the fees

01:04:20.570 --> 01:04:23.630 align:middle line:90%
that Google is charging.

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:27.080 align:middle line:84%
Note that a search produces
one unit of each of those

01:04:27.080 --> 01:04:28.350 align:middle line:90%
if the need is met.

01:04:28.350 --> 01:04:31.130 align:middle line:84%
Because if the need is
met, the consumer surplus

01:04:31.130 --> 01:04:33.470 align:middle line:84%
is 1 minus the
search cost incurred

01:04:33.470 --> 01:04:35.930 align:middle line:84%
so that the gross
consumer surplus is 1.

01:04:35.930 --> 01:04:38.990 align:middle line:84%
And the gross producer surplus
is the advertiser profit,

01:04:38.990 --> 01:04:41.533 align:middle line:84%
which is 1 minus the
search engine fees.

01:04:41.533 --> 01:04:42.950 align:middle line:84%
So if the search
is successful, it

01:04:42.950 --> 01:04:44.768 align:middle line:84%
produces one unit
of each of these.

01:04:44.768 --> 01:04:46.310 align:middle line:84%
And if it's
unsuccessful, it produces

01:04:46.310 --> 01:04:48.180 align:middle line:90%
0 units of each of these.

01:04:48.180 --> 01:04:52.620 align:middle line:84%
If you click and don't find
what you want, these are both 0.

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:56.300 align:middle line:84%
So that means the
expected gross consumer

01:04:56.300 --> 01:05:01.370 align:middle line:84%
surplus is the expected
gross producer surplus.

01:05:01.370 --> 01:05:05.330 align:middle line:84%
Welfare is gross consumer
surplus plus gross producer

01:05:05.330 --> 01:05:07.790 align:middle line:90%
surplus minus search costs.

01:05:07.790 --> 01:05:10.070 align:middle line:84%
So to prove the
theorem, we just need

01:05:10.070 --> 01:05:14.120 align:middle line:84%
to show that the expected
search costs are exactly half

01:05:14.120 --> 01:05:17.480 align:middle line:90%
of the gross consumer surplus.

01:05:17.480 --> 01:05:19.880 align:middle line:84%
But that follows immediately
from the optimality

01:05:19.880 --> 01:05:21.470 align:middle line:84%
of consumer search
and this assumption

01:05:21.470 --> 01:05:23.600 align:middle line:90%
that search costs are uniform.

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:25.280 align:middle line:84%
Because what's
going to happen is

01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:28.610 align:middle line:84%
every time an ad
is displayed, you

01:05:28.610 --> 01:05:32.450 align:middle line:84%
can think of who's going
to click on the ad.

01:05:32.450 --> 01:05:35.480 align:middle line:84%
The people with a low
search cost click on the ad.

01:05:35.480 --> 01:05:40.070 align:middle line:84%
The people with high search
costs don't click on the ad.

01:05:40.070 --> 01:05:41.430 align:middle line:90%
What's the cut off?

01:05:41.430 --> 01:05:45.380 align:middle line:84%
The cut off is
whatever information

01:05:45.380 --> 01:05:49.760 align:middle line:84%
you have, you're going to click
if the expected value of the ad,

01:05:49.760 --> 01:05:52.490 align:middle line:84%
given your information,
if your search cost

01:05:52.490 --> 01:05:54.440 align:middle line:84%
is less than the expected
value of clicking,

01:05:54.440 --> 01:05:56.310 align:middle line:84%
given the information you
have, you're going to click.

01:05:56.310 --> 01:05:58.020 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, you're
not going to click.

01:05:58.020 --> 01:06:02.160 align:middle line:84%
So what's the average
cost incurred by clicking?

01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:05.428 align:middle line:84%
It's just the expected
clicking cost conditional

01:06:05.428 --> 01:06:07.220 align:middle line:84%
on your clicking cost
being less than this,

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:12.398 align:middle line:84%
which is 1/2 of expected v. So
the expected value of search

01:06:12.398 --> 01:06:14.690 align:middle line:84%
costs, given that search
costs are less than a cut off,

01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:17.090 align:middle line:90%
is just 1/2 of that cut off.

01:06:17.090 --> 01:06:21.410 align:middle line:84%
So click by click, whatever all
these situations that come up,

01:06:21.410 --> 01:06:25.080 align:middle line:84%
the search costs are always half
of that conditional expectation.

01:06:25.080 --> 01:06:28.010 align:middle line:84%
So in average, search
costs eat up half

01:06:28.010 --> 01:06:29.350 align:middle line:90%
of the gross consumer surplus.

01:06:29.350 --> 01:06:36.630 align:middle line:90%


01:06:36.630 --> 01:06:38.980 align:middle line:84%
And so what does that
alignment theorem do?

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:42.330 align:middle line:84%
That alignment theorem,
so we have this.

01:06:42.330 --> 01:06:45.390 align:middle line:84%
Something that you can think
of, if we have of reserve prices

01:06:45.390 --> 01:06:51.030 align:middle line:84%
here on this axis, there's going
to be some amount of consumer

01:06:51.030 --> 01:06:54.060 align:middle line:90%
surplus that looks like this.

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:56.310 align:middle line:84%
And then there's just going
to be the producer surplus

01:06:56.310 --> 01:07:02.100 align:middle line:84%
is just going to
be 3 times that.

01:07:02.100 --> 01:07:05.370 align:middle line:84%
So producer surplus is
just always 3 times--

01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:11.980 align:middle line:84%
our producer surplus is just
always 3 times consumer surplus.

01:07:11.980 --> 01:07:16.650 align:middle line:84%
There's this unique
maximizing point r

01:07:16.650 --> 01:07:18.870 align:middle line:84%
star where consumer
surplus is maximized

01:07:18.870 --> 01:07:22.316 align:middle line:84%
and advertiser plus Google
profit is also maximized.

01:07:22.316 --> 01:07:25.907 align:middle line:90%


01:07:25.907 --> 01:07:27.740 align:middle line:84%
Simple example to show
that these things can

01:07:27.740 --> 01:07:29.990 align:middle line:84%
be positive is suppose
that there's only one

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:32.840 align:middle line:84%
slot on the screen and the
distributed search cost

01:07:32.840 --> 01:07:34.190 align:middle line:90%
is uniform.

01:07:34.190 --> 01:07:38.630 align:middle line:84%
Then the optimal reserve price
is always going to satisfy--

01:07:38.630 --> 01:07:42.560 align:middle line:84%
The reserve price is just 1/2 of
the expectation of the quality

01:07:42.560 --> 01:07:46.580 align:middle line:84%
of the best link, given that the
best link has quality at least

01:07:46.580 --> 01:07:47.450 align:middle line:90%
r.

01:07:47.450 --> 01:07:50.030 align:middle line:84%
Because it must be that if
you're maximizing consumer

01:07:50.030 --> 01:07:52.200 align:middle line:90%
surplus, it must be ex post.

01:07:52.200 --> 01:07:54.372 align:middle line:84%
When you're supposed
to display the link,

01:07:54.372 --> 01:07:55.830 align:middle line:84%
you're supposed to
display the link

01:07:55.830 --> 01:07:59.060 align:middle line:84%
if the bid is above r and not
display if the bid is below r.

01:07:59.060 --> 01:08:02.510 align:middle line:84%
It must be that's ex post
optimal for you to do.

01:08:02.510 --> 01:08:05.150 align:middle line:84%
If I display a link
and people think

01:08:05.150 --> 01:08:09.770 align:middle line:84%
that the quality is bigger than
r, this is going to be the--

01:08:09.770 --> 01:08:11.900 align:middle line:84%
people are going to
click if and only

01:08:11.900 --> 01:08:14.400 align:middle line:84%
if their value is
at least this high.

01:08:14.400 --> 01:08:17.260 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the search costs
incurred are going to be 1/2

01:08:17.260 --> 01:08:17.760 align:middle line:90%
of this.

01:08:17.760 --> 01:08:19.177 align:middle line:84%
And it has to be
that I don't want

01:08:19.177 --> 01:08:22.700 align:middle line:84%
to save the consumers from
search costs by not showing it.

01:08:22.700 --> 01:08:24.899 align:middle line:84%
And so I must be
indifferent of showing

01:08:24.899 --> 01:08:27.029 align:middle line:84%
an ad that has
quality exactly r,

01:08:27.029 --> 01:08:29.890 align:middle line:84%
which means that r
satisfies this equation.

01:08:29.890 --> 01:08:33.189 align:middle line:84%
So if I have one bidder, r is
going to be 1/3 or something

01:08:33.189 --> 01:08:33.689 align:middle line:90%
like that.

01:08:33.689 --> 01:08:35.564 align:middle line:84%
I do something kind of
like monopoly pricing,

01:08:35.564 --> 01:08:38.310 align:middle line:84%
and it's because
I don't want to--

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:41.010 align:middle line:84%
it has to be that consumers know
that this is my reserve price

01:08:41.010 --> 01:08:43.750 align:middle line:84%
and I only display things
that are this good.

01:08:43.750 --> 01:08:47.550 align:middle line:84%
Then once they know that
the quality is high,

01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:51.950 align:middle line:84%
their expected quality
has to be exactly twice r.

01:08:51.950 --> 01:08:59.010 align:middle line:90%


01:08:59.010 --> 01:09:03.660 align:middle line:84%
Another corollary is that
there is this inherent conflict

01:09:03.660 --> 01:09:06.689 align:middle line:84%
of interest between Google
and its advertisers that

01:09:06.689 --> 01:09:09.569 align:middle line:84%
says that advertiser surplus
is lower under the profit

01:09:09.569 --> 01:09:12.000 align:middle line:84%
maximizing reserve price
than under the consumer

01:09:12.000 --> 01:09:14.370 align:middle line:90%
optimal reserve price.

01:09:14.370 --> 01:09:18.000 align:middle line:84%
So under the consumer
optimal reserve price,

01:09:18.000 --> 01:09:20.500 align:middle line:90%
we get this consumer surplus.

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:22.710 align:middle line:84%
And this is the gross
advertiser surplus

01:09:22.710 --> 01:09:28.710 align:middle line:84%
shared between Google
and its advertisers.

01:09:28.710 --> 01:09:31.600 align:middle line:84%
Suppose an equilibrium Google
doesn't pick this reserve price.

01:09:31.600 --> 01:09:33.275 align:middle line:84%
Google picks a
higher reserve price.

01:09:33.275 --> 01:09:36.270 align:middle line:90%


01:09:36.270 --> 01:09:39.460 align:middle line:84%
So we had this one and this
one was shared between Google

01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:42.479 align:middle line:84%
gets some, the
advertisers get some.

01:09:42.479 --> 01:09:46.050 align:middle line:84%
If Google instead is picking
a different reserve price,

01:09:46.050 --> 01:09:48.960 align:middle line:84%
it must be that Google
is getting more profit

01:09:48.960 --> 01:09:51.609 align:middle line:84%
from this reserve price than
it gets at this reserve price.

01:09:51.609 --> 01:09:54.480 align:middle line:84%
So it must be that the
area of Google's profit

01:09:54.480 --> 01:09:57.670 align:middle line:84%
has gotten larger for Google
to want to choose that higher

01:09:57.670 --> 01:09:59.500 align:middle line:90%
reserve price.

01:09:59.500 --> 01:10:03.580 align:middle line:84%
But we know that consumer
surplus is lower.

01:10:03.580 --> 01:10:06.930 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, Google plus
producer surplus is lower.

01:10:06.930 --> 01:10:10.150 align:middle line:84%
So this is smaller than this
and Google is getting more,

01:10:10.150 --> 01:10:12.820 align:middle line:90%
so the advertisers get less.

01:10:12.820 --> 01:10:14.890 align:middle line:84%
So anything that
Google does that's

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:18.790 align:middle line:84%
different from the consumer
optimal reserve price

01:10:18.790 --> 01:10:20.980 align:middle line:84%
is something that's
hurting advertisers.

01:10:20.980 --> 01:10:21.730 align:middle line:90%
So there is this--

01:10:21.730 --> 01:10:25.540 align:middle line:90%


01:10:25.540 --> 01:10:28.705 align:middle line:84%
if your search engine is trying
to maximize consumer surplus

01:10:28.705 --> 01:10:31.330 align:middle line:84%
and then doing something other
than maximizing consumer surplus

01:10:31.330 --> 01:10:34.330 align:middle line:84%
is doing something that's worse
for advertisers than maximizing

01:10:34.330 --> 01:10:35.520 align:middle line:90%
consumer surplus is.

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:41.590 align:middle line:90%


01:10:41.590 --> 01:10:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And obviously in the long run,
I've talked to other people.

01:10:45.220 --> 01:10:47.260 align:middle line:84%
We think that it may
be reasonable to think

01:10:47.260 --> 01:10:49.030 align:middle line:84%
that in the early
days, Google was trying

01:10:49.030 --> 01:10:50.380 align:middle line:90%
to maximize consumer surplus.

01:10:50.380 --> 01:10:52.960 align:middle line:84%
If you're trying to maximize
the number of long run customers

01:10:52.960 --> 01:10:55.360 align:middle line:84%
using your platform,
it may be that trying

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:58.060 align:middle line:84%
to design the platform to
maximize this consumer surplus

01:10:58.060 --> 01:11:00.340 align:middle line:84%
is a good rule of
thumb for doing that.

01:11:00.340 --> 01:11:02.590 align:middle line:84%
But to the extent you start
monopoly pricing and doing

01:11:02.590 --> 01:11:04.340 align:middle line:84%
something other than
maximizing consumers.

01:11:04.340 --> 01:11:07.150 align:middle line:84%
So positive reserve prices
to maximize consumer surplus,

01:11:07.150 --> 01:11:09.130 align:middle line:90%
good for everyone.

01:11:09.130 --> 01:11:12.890 align:middle line:84%
Changing away from that, then
its advertisers are suffering.

01:11:12.890 --> 01:11:15.490 align:middle line:90%
Consumers are suffering.

01:11:15.490 --> 01:11:18.100 align:middle line:84%
Any departure from this,
advertisers and consumers

01:11:18.100 --> 01:11:20.670 align:middle line:84%
are both worse off if
Google is better off.

01:11:20.670 --> 01:11:27.310 align:middle line:90%


01:11:27.310 --> 01:11:29.440 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I just
think that this is

01:11:29.440 --> 01:11:34.900 align:middle line:84%
a literature that is still open
for people to do other things.

01:11:34.900 --> 01:11:36.640 align:middle line:84%
Search engine can
obviously do better

01:11:36.640 --> 01:11:38.563 align:middle line:90%
with more general strategies.

01:11:38.563 --> 01:11:40.480 align:middle line:84%
If you think about the
search engine is trying

01:11:40.480 --> 01:11:43.090 align:middle line:84%
to convey information,
you could really

01:11:43.090 --> 01:11:46.540 align:middle line:84%
bring in all kinds of
information design ideas.

01:11:46.540 --> 01:11:48.220 align:middle line:84%
You could think
about simple things

01:11:48.220 --> 01:11:52.100 align:middle line:84%
like reserve prices could vary
by the position on the screen.

01:11:52.100 --> 01:11:55.505 align:middle line:84%
The displays could convey more
information about quality.

01:11:55.505 --> 01:11:57.880 align:middle line:84%
I think Google already does
this by sometimes putting ads

01:11:57.880 --> 01:11:59.680 align:middle line:84%
only on the side
versus on the top

01:11:59.680 --> 01:12:02.260 align:middle line:84%
to give you some signal
of what the quality is.

01:12:02.260 --> 01:12:05.530 align:middle line:84%
But you could think about
giving more information somehow

01:12:05.530 --> 01:12:08.020 align:middle line:90%
about sponsored links.

01:12:08.020 --> 01:12:11.170 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the paper also
has sort of sections

01:12:11.170 --> 01:12:12.520 align:middle line:90%
about thinking about weighting.

01:12:12.520 --> 01:12:14.280 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense,
I think weighting--

01:12:14.280 --> 01:12:16.030 align:middle line:84%
Edelman, Ostrovsky,
Schwarz, every product

01:12:16.030 --> 01:12:17.822 align:middle line:84%
is independent of
every other product.

01:12:17.822 --> 01:12:19.780 align:middle line:84%
If you think about what
Google is trying to do,

01:12:19.780 --> 01:12:21.950 align:middle line:84%
Google is trying to
display a shopping mall.

01:12:21.950 --> 01:12:24.497 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to create a
shopping mall on the page.

01:12:24.497 --> 01:12:26.080 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
what they want to do

01:12:26.080 --> 01:12:28.520 align:middle line:84%
is also have some kind of
diversity weighting scheme,

01:12:28.520 --> 01:12:31.820 align:middle line:84%
like have something showing
women's shorts, some showing

01:12:31.820 --> 01:12:33.860 align:middle line:84%
men's shorts, some
showing athletic shorts,

01:12:33.860 --> 01:12:36.103 align:middle line:90%
some showing fashion shorts.

01:12:36.103 --> 01:12:37.520 align:middle line:84%
In some sense,
what you want to do

01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:39.290 align:middle line:84%
is have some
weights that reflect

01:12:39.290 --> 01:12:41.978 align:middle line:84%
how different every ad
is from every other ad

01:12:41.978 --> 01:12:43.520 align:middle line:84%
so that you're in
some sense creating

01:12:43.520 --> 01:12:44.895 align:middle line:84%
the equivalent of
a shopping mall

01:12:44.895 --> 01:12:47.840 align:middle line:84%
rather than maximizing
store by store by store

01:12:47.840 --> 01:12:50.640 align:middle line:84%
what you're trying
to do up there.

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:51.890 align:middle line:90%
OK, anyway, I will leave that.

01:12:51.890 --> 01:12:53.690 align:middle line:90%
People can read in the paper.

01:12:53.690 --> 01:12:56.450 align:middle line:90%
I have two other things here.

01:12:56.450 --> 01:12:59.690 align:middle line:84%
I will give you the 30
second version of each

01:12:59.690 --> 01:13:01.170 align:middle line:90%
of the following two papers.

01:13:01.170 --> 01:13:06.800 align:middle line:84%
So one recent paper, Anderson
and Renault, "Search Direction,

01:13:06.800 --> 01:13:09.240 align:middle line:84%
Positive Externalities,
and Position Auction Bias."

01:13:09.240 --> 01:13:16.220 align:middle line:84%
This is addressing a limitation
in all of these previous papers,

01:13:16.220 --> 01:13:22.790 align:middle line:84%
mine included, that in my paper,
we take prices as exogenous.

01:13:22.790 --> 01:13:24.950 align:middle line:84%
Every time you
click on an ad, you

01:13:24.950 --> 01:13:26.480 align:middle line:84%
get one unit of
consumer surplus,

01:13:26.480 --> 01:13:27.690 align:middle line:90%
one unit of producer surplus.

01:13:27.690 --> 01:13:29.730 align:middle line:84%
We don't let the
firms change prices.

01:13:29.730 --> 01:13:32.150 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine that
in a model like this,

01:13:32.150 --> 01:13:34.790 align:middle line:84%
firms are going to use
their pricing to exploit

01:13:34.790 --> 01:13:36.920 align:middle line:90%
their position on the screen.

01:13:36.920 --> 01:13:39.140 align:middle line:84%
And so what Anderson
and Renault work out

01:13:39.140 --> 01:13:41.540 align:middle line:84%
is what would happen
in a model where

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:44.390 align:middle line:90%
firms' pricing is endogenous?

01:13:44.390 --> 01:13:49.310 align:middle line:84%
And they can set in some sense,
the high price, the first firms,

01:13:49.310 --> 01:13:51.110 align:middle line:84%
they can exploit their
pricing advantage.

01:13:51.110 --> 01:13:54.350 align:middle line:84%
But they also may
want to use low prices

01:13:54.350 --> 01:13:56.642 align:middle line:84%
to keep consumers from
searching the things below them.

01:13:56.642 --> 01:13:58.600 align:middle line:84%
And in their model, it
turns out that turns out

01:13:58.600 --> 01:14:01.250 align:middle line:84%
to be the more important factor
that I want to set a price

01:14:01.250 --> 01:14:03.757 align:middle line:84%
low enough so that consumers
who like my product

01:14:03.757 --> 01:14:06.090 align:middle line:84%
don't have an incentive to
search the rest of the screen

01:14:06.090 --> 01:14:07.020 align:middle line:90%
and find the lower prices.

01:14:07.020 --> 01:14:09.440 align:middle line:84%
I want to take advantage of the
fact they've sunk their search

01:14:09.440 --> 01:14:10.982 align:middle line:84%
cost on me to get
them to buy from me

01:14:10.982 --> 01:14:13.760 align:middle line:90%
and not search everybody else.

01:14:13.760 --> 01:14:17.490 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, they have this model
where consumers have this need.

01:14:17.490 --> 01:14:20.060 align:middle line:84%
Every seller meets the
need with some probability.

01:14:20.060 --> 01:14:22.520 align:middle line:84%
But then if consumers
meet the need,

01:14:22.520 --> 01:14:24.860 align:middle line:84%
the gross utility
they will provide

01:14:24.860 --> 01:14:27.640 align:middle line:84%
is something distributed on
some interval v lower bar

01:14:27.640 --> 01:14:28.910 align:middle line:90%
v upper bar.

01:14:28.910 --> 01:14:32.860 align:middle line:84%
And then the firms set prices
optimally knowing their position

01:14:32.860 --> 01:14:33.560 align:middle line:90%
on the screen.

01:14:33.560 --> 01:14:36.070 align:middle line:84%
So once they learn their
position on the screen,

01:14:36.070 --> 01:14:38.630 align:middle line:90%
they set their price.

01:14:38.630 --> 01:14:40.540 align:middle line:84%
And the interesting
aspect of this paper

01:14:40.540 --> 01:14:45.670 align:middle line:84%
is they note that this model
has multiple equilibria.

01:14:45.670 --> 01:14:48.400 align:middle line:84%
Even if you have
just two firms, you

01:14:48.400 --> 01:14:51.040 align:middle line:84%
can have the low quality firm
on top and the high quality firm

01:14:51.040 --> 01:14:53.770 align:middle line:84%
below or the high quality firm
on top and the low quality firm

01:14:53.770 --> 01:14:54.620 align:middle line:90%
below.

01:14:54.620 --> 01:14:57.500 align:middle line:84%
And they can both be equilibria
with consumers searching top

01:14:57.500 --> 01:14:58.000 align:middle line:90%
down.

01:14:58.000 --> 01:15:01.270 align:middle line:84%
Because in each
case, all the firms

01:15:01.270 --> 01:15:02.990 align:middle line:84%
know that the consumers
search top down.

01:15:02.990 --> 01:15:05.350 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the top firm
sets a really low price

01:15:05.350 --> 01:15:09.280 align:middle line:84%
to keep consumers from searching
the second one, and therefore,

01:15:09.280 --> 01:15:11.440 align:middle line:84%
having either firm
on top at a low price

01:15:11.440 --> 01:15:13.065 align:middle line:84%
and then the firm
below at a high price

01:15:13.065 --> 01:15:14.773 align:middle line:84%
to only serve consumers
who couldn't meet

01:15:14.773 --> 01:15:16.130 align:middle line:90%
their need at the first firm.

01:15:16.130 --> 01:15:18.100 align:middle line:90%
Those are both equilibria.

01:15:18.100 --> 01:15:20.950 align:middle line:84%
And so they work out that
this becomes a model where

01:15:20.950 --> 01:15:22.720 align:middle line:90%
that has multiple equilibria.

01:15:22.720 --> 01:15:24.850 align:middle line:84%
And different
equilibria are better

01:15:24.850 --> 01:15:27.530 align:middle line:84%
for consumers and different
equilibria are better for firms,

01:15:27.530 --> 01:15:30.810 align:middle line:84%
and they all involve
this endogenous pricing.

01:15:30.810 --> 01:15:34.160 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's an interesting
recent working paper.

01:15:34.160 --> 01:15:37.550 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
one I wanted to mention

01:15:37.550 --> 01:15:42.410 align:middle line:84%
is Armstrong and Zhou
on consumer information

01:15:42.410 --> 01:15:44.220 align:middle line:90%
in platform design.

01:15:44.220 --> 01:15:54.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is thinking about
Amazon or other platforms who

01:15:54.350 --> 01:15:58.220 align:middle line:84%
are mediating the process
through which outside sellers

01:15:58.220 --> 01:16:01.070 align:middle line:90%
sell products to consumers.

01:16:01.070 --> 01:16:03.770 align:middle line:84%
And the thought is going
to be that consumers

01:16:03.770 --> 01:16:06.740 align:middle line:84%
are going to get
utility vij minus pj

01:16:06.740 --> 01:16:10.670 align:middle line:84%
if they buy from Firm J, where
vij is a random variable that

01:16:10.670 --> 01:16:13.100 align:middle line:84%
represents your idiosyncratic
preferences for the two

01:16:13.100 --> 01:16:14.600 align:middle line:90%
firms' products.

01:16:14.600 --> 01:16:18.440 align:middle line:84%
And the platform design consists
of choosing and committing

01:16:18.440 --> 01:16:20.103 align:middle line:90%
to some signal structure.

01:16:20.103 --> 01:16:22.520 align:middle line:84%
How much are you going to tell
the consumers about the two

01:16:22.520 --> 01:16:24.860 align:middle line:90%
products?

01:16:24.860 --> 01:16:28.400 align:middle line:84%
How much are you going to tell
the consumers about two products

01:16:28.400 --> 01:16:29.900 align:middle line:90%
before they click on the ads?

01:16:29.900 --> 01:16:33.680 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, by limiting how
much information consumers get,

01:16:33.680 --> 01:16:36.110 align:middle line:84%
you change the price
competition between firms.

01:16:36.110 --> 01:16:39.530 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is an
endogenous pricing paper.

01:16:39.530 --> 01:16:45.890 align:middle line:84%
And first immediate thought
is that the consumer optimal

01:16:45.890 --> 01:16:47.720 align:middle line:90%
outcome is unattainable.

01:16:47.720 --> 01:16:49.820 align:middle line:84%
What you would
like to do is have

01:16:49.820 --> 01:16:54.500 align:middle line:84%
all consumers buy the product
they like better at p equals c.

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:57.140 align:middle line:84%
But having all consumers get the
product they like better at p

01:16:57.140 --> 01:17:00.770 align:middle line:84%
equals c is not feasible,
because for consumers

01:17:00.770 --> 01:17:02.270 align:middle line:84%
to get the product
they like better,

01:17:02.270 --> 01:17:04.940 align:middle line:84%
they have to know which
product they like better.

01:17:04.940 --> 01:17:08.300 align:middle line:84%
And if you tell consumers
which product they like better,

01:17:08.300 --> 01:17:11.660 align:middle line:84%
then the firms are going to
have market power knowing

01:17:11.660 --> 01:17:14.480 align:middle line:84%
that all the consumers know
that they like my product better

01:17:14.480 --> 01:17:16.272 align:middle line:84%
than the other firm's
product and are going

01:17:16.272 --> 01:17:19.520 align:middle line:90%
to price that into their--

01:17:19.520 --> 01:17:22.110 align:middle line:84%
they're going to raise
their price to reflect that.

01:17:22.110 --> 01:17:24.320 align:middle line:84%
So for consumers to get the
product they like better,

01:17:24.320 --> 01:17:25.790 align:middle line:84%
they have to know which
one they like better.

01:17:25.790 --> 01:17:27.260 align:middle line:90%
Then there's differentiation.

01:17:27.260 --> 01:17:31.730 align:middle line:84%
Then the pricing game results
in pricing not at marginal cost.

01:17:31.730 --> 01:17:37.130 align:middle line:84%
So there's no way to get that
fully consumer optimal outcome.

01:17:37.130 --> 01:17:40.160 align:middle line:84%
The seller optimal outcome
you can sometimes do.

01:17:40.160 --> 01:17:43.007 align:middle line:84%
The seller optimal outcome
is to tell consumers

01:17:43.007 --> 01:17:44.840 align:middle line:84%
which product they like
better and give them

01:17:44.840 --> 01:17:48.740 align:middle line:84%
0 other information about
how much they like it.

01:17:48.740 --> 01:17:51.530 align:middle line:84%
Because they have no information
about how much better it is,

01:17:51.530 --> 01:17:54.170 align:middle line:84%
then all consumers
are ex ante identical,

01:17:54.170 --> 01:17:58.022 align:middle line:84%
and the firms can just charge
consumers what is your value.

01:17:58.022 --> 01:17:59.480 align:middle line:84%
If you know there
are two products,

01:17:59.480 --> 01:18:00.890 align:middle line:84%
you know which one
you like better,

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:03.182 align:middle line:84%
what's your expected value
for the one you like better?

01:18:03.182 --> 01:18:05.203 align:middle line:84%
And then fully extract
the consumer surplus.

01:18:05.203 --> 01:18:08.660 align:middle line:90%


01:18:08.660 --> 01:18:10.080 align:middle line:84%
And so that's
sometimes possible.

01:18:10.080 --> 01:18:14.720 align:middle line:84%
And then they work
out, what are you

01:18:14.720 --> 01:18:16.220 align:middle line:84%
going to want to
do if you're trying

01:18:16.220 --> 01:18:19.443 align:middle line:84%
to maximize consumer surplus
versus producer surplus?

01:18:19.443 --> 01:18:22.050 align:middle line:90%


01:18:22.050 --> 01:18:27.950 align:middle line:84%
And what they show
is that if you--

01:18:27.950 --> 01:18:30.750 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, this is going
back to this information design

01:18:30.750 --> 01:18:31.250 align:middle line:90%
thing.

01:18:31.250 --> 01:18:34.670 align:middle line:84%
We are just saying what is
going to be the posterior?

01:18:34.670 --> 01:18:39.530 align:middle line:84%
What is going to be consumer's
posterior on v1 minus v2 given

01:18:39.530 --> 01:18:43.100 align:middle line:84%
whatever information
signal you've given them?

01:18:43.100 --> 01:18:47.870 align:middle line:84%
And what you want to do to
maximize consumer surplus

01:18:47.870 --> 01:18:56.460 align:middle line:84%
is have consumers who view
the two products differently.

01:18:56.460 --> 01:18:58.370 align:middle line:84%
I think I like product
one slightly more

01:18:58.370 --> 01:19:01.440 align:middle line:84%
than product two or product two
slightly more than product one.

01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:04.152 align:middle line:84%
And you want to have them
have a posterior like this

01:19:04.152 --> 01:19:06.110 align:middle line:84%
that creates tremendous
incentive for the firms

01:19:06.110 --> 01:19:06.740 align:middle line:90%
to undercut.

01:19:06.740 --> 01:19:09.390 align:middle line:84%
Because most consumers
know that I like firm

01:19:09.390 --> 01:19:11.880 align:middle line:84%
one only epsilon better than
firm two, given the epsilon

01:19:11.880 --> 01:19:13.360 align:middle line:90%
information you've given me.

01:19:13.360 --> 01:19:15.480 align:middle line:84%
And so by giving them
this epsilon information

01:19:15.480 --> 01:19:19.230 align:middle line:84%
about how much they like
product A versus product B, then

01:19:19.230 --> 01:19:24.370 align:middle line:84%
you've created very
high elasticities.

01:19:24.370 --> 01:19:26.890 align:middle line:90%
And so the firms price very low.

01:19:26.890 --> 01:19:28.367 align:middle line:84%
And even only
having this thinking

01:19:28.367 --> 01:19:30.700 align:middle line:84%
that you like this one epsilon
better than the other one

01:19:30.700 --> 01:19:34.110 align:middle line:84%
in expectation lets you
often get the right product.

01:19:34.110 --> 01:19:36.960 align:middle line:84%
So giving consumers this
minimal information about--

01:19:36.960 --> 01:19:40.050 align:middle line:84%
lots of consumers, minimal
information about which product

01:19:40.050 --> 01:19:43.320 align:middle line:84%
they like better creates
very intense competition

01:19:43.320 --> 01:19:45.780 align:middle line:84%
and makes that trade off of
getting a lot of consumers

01:19:45.780 --> 01:19:49.620 align:middle line:84%
to buy the product
they do like better.

01:19:49.620 --> 01:19:53.850 align:middle line:84%
Whereas then the profit
maximizing solution,

01:19:53.850 --> 01:19:57.450 align:middle line:84%
profit maximizing
solution is often to--

01:19:57.450 --> 01:20:03.600 align:middle line:84%
you give consumers--
many consumers

01:20:03.600 --> 01:20:05.460 align:middle line:84%
think they like one
product much more

01:20:05.460 --> 01:20:07.800 align:middle line:84%
than the other, and
only a few consumers who

01:20:07.800 --> 01:20:08.790 align:middle line:84%
think that they're
roughly indifferent

01:20:08.790 --> 01:20:09.748 align:middle line:90%
between their products.

01:20:09.748 --> 01:20:12.630 align:middle line:84%
And you do that to try to get
the prices as high as you can

01:20:12.630 --> 01:20:14.670 align:middle line:84%
possibly get and get
many of the consumers

01:20:14.670 --> 01:20:16.680 align:middle line:90%
to buy the products they like.

01:20:16.680 --> 01:20:20.670 align:middle line:84%
But I guess one of the
takeaways from this paper

01:20:20.670 --> 01:20:23.130 align:middle line:84%
was that they found
that there's really not

01:20:23.130 --> 01:20:28.080 align:middle line:84%
much scope to transfer
surplus between consumers.

01:20:28.080 --> 01:20:30.160 align:middle line:84%
Not much scope to
modify social welfare.

01:20:30.160 --> 01:20:33.040 align:middle line:84%
A lot of it is transferring
surplus between consumers

01:20:33.040 --> 01:20:35.040 align:middle line:84%
and producers by giving
this kind of information

01:20:35.040 --> 01:20:36.670 align:middle line:90%
or this kind of information.

01:20:36.670 --> 01:20:41.850 align:middle line:84%
But it's harder to modify the
equilibrium social welfare.

01:20:41.850 --> 01:20:46.050 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, if you're interested in
reading recent papers on Google

01:20:46.050 --> 01:20:49.800 align:middle line:84%
with pricing or on Amazon, these
would be two I'd recommend.

01:20:49.800 --> 01:20:53.830 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, Monday I'm
going to be back here.

01:20:53.830 --> 01:20:56.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be doing empirical
work on online markets.

01:20:56.370 --> 01:21:00.630 align:middle line:84%
And these will be actually
largely papers on online retail

01:21:00.630 --> 01:21:03.980 align:middle line:84%
and thinking about consumer
preferences and online retail.

01:21:03.980 --> 01:21:17.000 align:middle line:90%